WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.610 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.440 align:middle line:84%
The following
content is provided

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.340 align:middle line:84%
by MIT OpenCourseWare under
a Creative Commons license.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.470 align:middle line:84%
Additional information
about our license

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:10.870 align:middle line:84%
and MIT OpenCourseWare
in general

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.730 align:middle line:90%
is available at ocw.mit.edu.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:17.170 align:middle line:90%


00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:20.410 align:middle line:90%
GEORGE CHURCH: OK, ready.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.090 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:29.238 align:middle line:84%
So welcome to the third phase
of our omics discussion.

00:00:29.238 --> 00:00:31.030 align:middle line:84%
Someone pointed out
that I should point out

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:35.110 align:middle line:84%
[? this shirt ?] says
omics not comics.

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:39.340 align:middle line:84%
So we've covered genomics and
last time transcriptomics,

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:43.000 align:middle line:84%
and today we introduce a very
important all-inclusive subject

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.160 align:middle line:90%
of proteomics.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.820 align:middle line:84%
We'll connect it to last
week's through the vehicle

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:53.800 align:middle line:84%
of focusing on motifs
that are involved

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:58.450 align:middle line:84%
in protein interactions
with the two nucleic acid

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.610 align:middle line:90%
macromolecules.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:04.540 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to be covering,
just as we introduced

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.650 align:middle line:84%
RNA omics with RNA
structure, we're

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:11.260 align:middle line:84%
going to spend this entire class
talking about protein three

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.410 align:middle line:84%
dimensional structure, how
you get at it experimentally

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:17.050 align:middle line:84%
and computationally
and its implications

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:22.060 align:middle line:84%
for the binding of small
molecules such as drugs.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:27.550 align:middle line:84%
We will in short order get
to the scary pumpkin-like

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:29.450 align:middle line:90%
molecule.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:38.980 align:middle line:84%
So the connection to last
week was this diagram

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:43.360 align:middle line:84%
showing palindromicity in
three cases and a direct repeat

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.880 align:middle line:90%
in the fourth case.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.490 align:middle line:84%
And I offered that
this might reflect

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.310 align:middle line:84%
the symmetry of the
proteins-- of the three

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.320 align:middle line:84%
dimensional structure
of the proteins

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.930 align:middle line:84%
and the three dimensional
structure of the nucleic acid

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:57.910 align:middle line:84%
and these symmetry
elements would align.

00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:00.220 align:middle line:84%
Now in order to introduce
these symmetry elements

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:02.370 align:middle line:84%
and the possibility
of having codes

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:05.140 align:middle line:84%
that you can at
least program, even

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:08.065 align:middle line:84%
if they may have been tinkered
about during an evolution,

00:02:08.065 --> 00:02:09.880 align:middle line:84%
the question is to
what extent can we

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.920 align:middle line:84%
get our hands on these kind of
protein and nucleic acid motifs

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.370 align:middle line:90%
that interact.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:19.450 align:middle line:84%
In order to get at this issue
of where there is a code--

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.870 align:middle line:84%
and I just take this as
one of the ways of dealing

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.840 align:middle line:84%
with the incredible
complexity of proteins

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.590 align:middle line:84%
is to give this a theme that
connects it to the last class

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:32.560 align:middle line:84%
and connects, I think, to many
of the sentiments of people

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.900 align:middle line:84%
in this sort of
audience interested

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:39.820 align:middle line:84%
in computational biology is
ways of having simple codes.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:41.980 align:middle line:84%
And of course,
the way of one way

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:44.050 align:middle line:84%
of breaking up proteins
and thinking about them

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:49.060 align:middle line:84%
is these ABCs-- the alpha helix,
the beta sheet, and the coil.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:51.700 align:middle line:84%
Each of these can be
characterized by the hydrogen

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.230 align:middle line:90%
bonds that hold it together.

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:57.460 align:middle line:84%
The weak bonds between the
hydrogens, the nitrogens,

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.103 align:middle line:90%
and the oxygens.

00:02:59.103 --> 00:03:00.520 align:middle line:84%
And the alpha
helix, these are all

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:03.280 align:middle line:84%
kept within the
helix with a repeat

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.090 align:middle line:90%
of 3.6 residues per turn.

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:12.880 align:middle line:84%
In the beta sheet, they tend
to have a longer, straighter

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:17.260 align:middle line:84%
chains where there are unpaired
hydrogen bonds inevitably

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.660 align:middle line:84%
until you form
enough chains to form

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:22.480 align:middle line:84%
a cyclic structure while the
alpha helix is immediately

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:25.103 align:middle line:90%
helical.

00:03:25.103 --> 00:03:26.770 align:middle line:84%
There are many different
types of coils.

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.770 align:middle line:84%
It's a catch-all phrase
that includes everything

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:33.340 align:middle line:84%
except alpha and beta, but a
particularly well-formed type

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:38.530 align:middle line:84%
of coil has its own nomenclature
and parameters is the turn,

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:44.110 align:middle line:84%
and the turn is illustrated
here at the end of a beta sheet.

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:45.580 align:middle line:84%
That beta sheet
basically is going

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:47.920 align:middle line:84%
in on the lower
right hand corner

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:49.300 align:middle line:90%
in the direction of the arrow.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.820 align:middle line:84%
The arrows typically
point from the n terminus

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:55.000 align:middle line:84%
to the c terminus just
as in nucleic acids

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.680 align:middle line:84%
is from five prime
to three prime.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:58.260 align:middle line:84%
And here it goes
in the arrow, it

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:02.080 align:middle line:84%
turns around very tightly,
and goes back out again.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:06.100 align:middle line:84%
OK, now how can we use
these basic motifs?

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:10.570 align:middle line:84%
These are the smallest
meaningful units of protein

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:11.750 align:middle line:90%
three dimensional structure.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:13.540 align:middle line:84%
How can we use
these to recognize

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:17.480 align:middle line:84%
other macromolecules, other
proteins and nucleic acids?

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:20.149 align:middle line:84%
So let's connect this to
the motifs of last class.

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:23.020 align:middle line:84%
We have these motifs
that we could find,

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:26.200 align:middle line:84%
weight matrices for them by
aligning lots of sequences.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:27.720 align:middle line:84%
Now instead of
aligning sequences,

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:33.880 align:middle line:84%
let's see what we can do by
mutating both the protein

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:36.070 align:middle line:90%
part and the nucleic acid part.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:39.340 align:middle line:84%
And in order to do this,
just as an illustration,

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:40.900 align:middle line:84%
let's say we have
three zinc fingers.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:47.770 align:middle line:84%
This is a real human and
mouse DNA binding protein

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:49.310 align:middle line:84%
with three zinc
fingers in a row.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:51.910 align:middle line:84%
So this is an example of the
direct repeat or tandem repeat

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:53.020 align:middle line:90%
type of symmetry.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:55.780 align:middle line:84%
Remember, there's the direct
repeat and the inverted repeat.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:58.390 align:middle line:84%
And in this tandem repeat,
let's anchor the two ends

00:04:58.390 --> 00:04:59.710 align:middle line:90%
and change the middle.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:01.630 align:middle line:84%
Make every possible
peptide sequence

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:06.160 align:middle line:84%
in the middle or randomly
sample the vast space that

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:13.060 align:middle line:84%
might occur in changing
a few, say, six or more

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:14.680 align:middle line:90%
key amino acids.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:18.130 align:middle line:84%
And then we know from the
three dimensional structure

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:21.430 align:middle line:84%
that it interacts mainly with
the middle three nucleotides,

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:22.930 align:middle line:84%
so let's change
those middle three

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:26.200 align:middle line:84%
nucleotides to every possible
trinucleotide sequence

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:32.020 align:middle line:84%
and see quantitatively how much
that the different sequences

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:34.450 align:middle line:84%
in the protein affect
the different sequences

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:36.050 align:middle line:90%
in the nucleic acid.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:39.477 align:middle line:84%
So this is not going to be
by staring at long sequence

00:05:39.477 --> 00:05:41.810 align:middle line:84%
alignments where we're going
to get the weight matrices.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.290 align:middle line:84%
We can get them by actual
experimental measures

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:47.233 align:middle line:90%
of the binding in vitro.

00:05:47.233 --> 00:05:49.150 align:middle line:84%
And so what happens when
you do this exercise,

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:51.700 align:middle line:84%
the wild type, now this--
the wild type sequence

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:55.210 align:middle line:84%
is something that
may want to recognize

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:56.260 align:middle line:90%
a family of sequences.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:59.080 align:middle line:84%
We don't know exactly what
the wild type sequence

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.540 align:middle line:84%
is for this particular DNA
binding protein, this zinc

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:03.560 align:middle line:90%
finger from human and mouse.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:05.770 align:middle line:84%
But the subsequence
of the business end,

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:09.490 align:middle line:84%
the amino acid subsequence of
that recognition alpha helix

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:11.950 align:middle line:84%
that's binding into
the major groove DNA

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:15.850 align:middle line:84%
is shown in the upper
left, [? RSVHLTT. ?]

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:18.268 align:middle line:84%
And the sequence that it
mainly binds to-- remember this

00:06:18.268 --> 00:06:19.060 align:middle line:90%
is a weight matrix.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:22.450 align:middle line:84%
It's not a consensus
sequence, this TGG.

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:24.850 align:middle line:84%
It obviously recognizes a
variety of other sequences.

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:27.340 align:middle line:84%
So remember, there's
about three nucleotides

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:30.850 align:middle line:84%
on either side of it that the
other two zinc fingers bind to.

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:35.197 align:middle line:84%
When you try all 64 possible
trinucleotides-- remember

00:06:35.197 --> 00:06:37.155 align:middle line:84%
from the genetic code,
this is pure coincidence

00:06:37.155 --> 00:06:39.700 align:middle line:84%
that these are triplets the
same as the genetic code.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:42.070 align:middle line:84%
This just happens to be the
amount of the chunk of DNA

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:45.460 align:middle line:90%
that a zinc finger will cover.

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:47.740 align:middle line:84%
But it's not coincidence
that 4 to the third

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:50.530 align:middle line:84%
is still 64, just
like the genetic code.

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:54.940 align:middle line:84%
And if you run through all
possible nucleotide sequences

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:58.750 align:middle line:84%
for this wild type, you
find the winner is TGG,

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:00.670 align:middle line:84%
and it has that particular
binding constant.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:04.330 align:middle line:84%
The binding constant is
measured in the molarity,

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:09.070 align:middle line:84%
roughly where you get half
maximal or equilibrium binding.

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:12.970 align:middle line:84%
That's 10 to the minus
9 moles per liter.

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:16.990 align:middle line:84%
You can now mutagenize the
peptide and select for peptides

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:18.970 align:middle line:90%
that bind to GCC.

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:21.470 align:middle line:84%
Remember, the flanks
are kept constant.

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:24.250 align:middle line:84%
You get two peptides,
both bind to the GCC.

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:29.830 align:middle line:84%
They both give matrices very
similar to this with very--

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:31.960 align:middle line:84%
these are very high
affinity binding constants

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:33.220 align:middle line:90%
just like wild type.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:39.570 align:middle line:84%
You've essentially engineered
by selection a new specificity

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:41.700 align:middle line:84%
and two different
ways of getting it.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:44.040 align:middle line:84%
If you now go for something
radically different,

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:45.540 align:middle line:90%
now no GC--

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:49.140 align:middle line:84%
the first one was high GC,
the second was pure GC,

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:51.090 align:middle line:84%
and then the third
one is pure AT.

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:54.083 align:middle line:84%
And you get another peptide
sequence that binds to that,

00:07:54.083 --> 00:07:55.500 align:middle line:84%
and you get another
weight matrix.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.958 align:middle line:84%
Now remember, this weight matrix
is not sequence alignment.

00:07:57.958 --> 00:08:03.000 align:middle line:84%
This is binding constants
where the weighting of the 64

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.030 align:middle line:84%
different sequences is based
on how much binding you

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:07.800 align:middle line:90%
get for each of the 64.

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:11.550 align:middle line:84%
And then for some sequences, all
these different trinucleotides

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:14.430 align:middle line:84%
all result in a
rather poor selection

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:17.640 align:middle line:84%
for any kind of peptide out of
all the vast number of peptides

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:18.180 align:middle line:90%
you have.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:19.890 align:middle line:84%
None of them do
particularly well,

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:23.590 align:middle line:84%
and the result is a
weight matrix here

00:08:23.590 --> 00:08:25.740 align:middle line:84%
which has very little
information content.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.593 align:middle line:90%
So this is a way.

00:08:27.593 --> 00:08:29.010 align:middle line:84%
It's not the only
way, but this is

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:33.059 align:middle line:84%
a way of getting a really
good empirical data set, which

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:36.419 align:middle line:84%
in principle, you can combine
it with similar functions

00:08:36.419 --> 00:08:39.690 align:middle line:84%
on the flanking ones,
and you can dial up

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:44.700 align:middle line:84%
any sequence of a nucleic acid
protein interaction, at least

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:45.900 align:middle line:90%
with this class of proteins.

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:48.450 align:middle line:84%
Others are a little
more problematic.

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:50.850 align:middle line:84%
But you can see how this
can generate a code even

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:55.980 align:middle line:84%
if the actual detailed
amino acid nucleotide

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:59.770 align:middle line:90%
interaction is not so simple.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:02.010 align:middle line:84%
So that those are the
results of the study.

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:04.020 align:middle line:84%
Then I'll show you
just a snapshot

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:07.245 align:middle line:84%
of a schematic of how the
actual experiments are done.

00:09:07.245 --> 00:09:09.120 align:middle line:84%
And then finally, I'll
show you a little math

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:12.330 align:middle line:84%
behind how we got those
apparent binding constants.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:16.470 align:middle line:84%
Remember, in those lower
binding constant means

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:19.750 align:middle line:84%
you can get the binding
at a lower concentration,

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.180 align:middle line:90%
which means a stronger binding.

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:23.670 align:middle line:84%
So the way you do
the binding here

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:27.420 align:middle line:84%
is you take a
nucleic acid array,

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:30.330 align:middle line:84%
similar to the ones we've been
talking about in last class.

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:32.670 align:middle line:84%
Instead of being single
stranded, ready to bind

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:34.800 align:middle line:84%
fluorescent nucleic acid,
it's double stranded,

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:38.040 align:middle line:84%
ready to bind a fluorescent
protein complex.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:43.290 align:middle line:84%
The protein complex in this
case is a bacteriophage,

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:46.320 align:middle line:84%
which is displaying the
three zinc fingers in red.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:51.180 align:middle line:84%
The middle one is the one in
the past slide was mutagenized.

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:54.690 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, the
array is combinatoric--

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:57.540 align:middle line:84%
every possible DNA sequence
that you're interested in

00:09:57.540 --> 00:09:58.350 align:middle line:90%
is present.

00:09:58.350 --> 00:10:04.470 align:middle line:84%
And what you do is quantitate
the fluorescence of the zinc

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:08.670 align:middle line:84%
finger protein
indirectly by the binding

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:12.660 align:middle line:84%
of the covalently-attached
phage to the antibodies, which

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.097 align:middle line:84%
are fluorescently labeled
by [INAUDIBLE] fluorescence.

00:10:15.097 --> 00:10:17.840 align:middle line:90%


00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:21.410 align:middle line:84%
The quantity-- how you relate
the fluorescence, the more

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:23.060 align:middle line:90%
binding, the more fluorescence.

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:25.490 align:middle line:84%
But how you relate that
to the binding constant

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:28.340 align:middle line:84%
we had in the previous
slide is the subject

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:31.070 align:middle line:90%
of this slide number eight.

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:35.660 align:middle line:84%
Now we call this the apparent
equilibrium association

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:37.580 align:middle line:84%
constant because
these experiments,

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:43.850 align:middle line:84%
just like many binding in living
cells is not at equilibrium.

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:48.740 align:middle line:84%
It's a dynamic process
in the cell and in vitro.

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:51.560 align:middle line:84%
There are ways that you
can measure the equilibrium

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:54.680 align:middle line:84%
constants, but what this
is apparent in the sense

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:57.500 align:middle line:84%
that you need to wash off the
excess fluorescence in order

00:10:57.500 --> 00:10:59.990 align:middle line:84%
to detect the fairly
low signal that you

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:03.238 align:middle line:84%
get from the specific
binding, rather

00:11:03.238 --> 00:11:04.280 align:middle line:90%
than having fluorescence.

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:08.870 align:middle line:84%
You bathe it with 10 to the
sixford excess of fluorescence.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:10.610 align:middle line:84%
And so as you're
doing that wash,

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:12.110 align:middle line:84%
you're obviously
not at equilibrium.

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:17.060 align:middle line:84%
In the end, you take a snapshot
before you wash off everything.

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:20.360 align:middle line:84%
And so what you're measuring--
what you're basically measuring

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:21.290 align:middle line:90%
is--

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:23.390 align:middle line:84%
attempting to measure is
the equilibrium constant

00:11:23.390 --> 00:11:26.120 align:middle line:84%
between protein P
in the upper left

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:29.900 align:middle line:84%
here, DNA D, double
stranded going

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:35.210 align:middle line:84%
to the product, which is this
P.D associated biomolecular

00:11:35.210 --> 00:11:36.230 align:middle line:90%
product.

00:11:36.230 --> 00:11:42.230 align:middle line:84%
And this is basic physical
chemistry and algebra

00:11:42.230 --> 00:11:44.600 align:middle line:84%
so that-- so you
rearrange that to get

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:46.500 align:middle line:90%
the association constant.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:47.570 align:middle line:90%
That's what you want.

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:49.910 align:middle line:84%
And the fraction of DNA
molecules with the protein

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:52.910 align:middle line:90%
bounds can be found from this.

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:58.460 align:middle line:84%
It's just by definition, the
fraction DNA molecules protein

00:11:58.460 --> 00:11:59.810 align:middle line:90%
bound is--

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:02.040 align:middle line:84%
the protein bound
is the numerator.

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:04.610 align:middle line:84%
The brackets mean concentration
in moles per liter,

00:12:04.610 --> 00:12:06.110 align:middle line:90%
just like we stated.

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:09.980 align:middle line:84%
And then divide the
complex by the amount

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:13.940 align:middle line:84%
of the fraction, which
is the total DNA, which

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:16.740 align:middle line:84%
is the DNA present in the
complex and the DNA is free.

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:19.340 align:middle line:90%
That's the D plus PD.

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:22.160 align:middle line:84%
And then you just substitute
in this definition

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:23.870 align:middle line:90%
of the association constant.

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:29.460 align:middle line:84%
The definition is
product over reactants,

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:31.720 align:middle line:84%
and then you get this
intermediate term.

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:34.770 align:middle line:84%
Which now you cancel out all
the concentrations of these,

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:37.690 align:middle line:84%
and then you end up with the
last term on the far right,

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:41.760 align:middle line:84%
which is where if you hold
the protein-- if the protein

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:44.850 align:middle line:84%
concentration is known,
then the whole thing is

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:47.010 align:middle line:84%
constant except for
the association.

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:49.860 align:middle line:84%
So that one over the
association constant

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:53.400 align:middle line:84%
is directly related to the
fraction of DNA molecules

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:56.220 align:middle line:84%
with protein bound,
which is directly

00:12:56.220 --> 00:13:00.630 align:middle line:84%
proportional to the signal
intensity and fluorescence.

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:02.550 align:middle line:84%
So this is how you
get the numbers that

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:03.930 align:middle line:90%
were on that slide.

00:13:03.930 --> 00:13:06.450 align:middle line:84%
Now let's return
the question that

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:08.460 align:middle line:84%
connected this talk
with the last one, which

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:15.210 align:middle line:84%
was the symmetry of DNA
protein interactions.

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:19.500 align:middle line:84%
We illustrated already one
of these three zinc finger

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:26.850 align:middle line:84%
complexes, as illustrated
on the left hand side here.

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:31.260 align:middle line:84%
The double-stranded DNA is in
blue and the three zinc fingers

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:36.820 align:middle line:84%
follow along the major groove,
the large groove of the DNA.

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:40.320 align:middle line:84%
And the reason the textbook
is wrong, first of all,

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:42.858 align:middle line:84%
it emphasizes the non-helical
part of the zinc finger.

00:13:42.858 --> 00:13:45.150 align:middle line:84%
You can barely see the helix
with the background there.

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:47.392 align:middle line:84%
And also the way it
loops through the DNA,

00:13:47.392 --> 00:13:49.350 align:middle line:84%
if you look at this
carefully in your textbook,

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:51.420 align:middle line:84%
this is actually the
[? Mount ?] book,

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:54.405 align:middle line:84%
it actually interdigitates
with a phosphodiester bond,

00:13:54.405 --> 00:13:56.280 align:middle line:84%
basically going through
the base pairs, which

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:58.380 align:middle line:90%
is not at all what happens.

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:00.600 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, the
leucine zipper, this

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:02.910 align:middle line:84%
is, again, recognition
with a helix

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:05.850 align:middle line:90%
in the major groove of DNA.

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:08.490 align:middle line:84%
Here, you can see
that the helix that

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:10.500 align:middle line:84%
causes the dimerization
of proteins-- you

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:14.400 align:middle line:84%
can think of this as your really
most elementary protein-protein

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:15.480 align:middle line:90%
interaction code.

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:18.090 align:middle line:84%
A very fundamental one
that comes again and again,

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:23.220 align:middle line:84%
so-called coil-coil, two
alpha helices interacting.

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:26.940 align:middle line:84%
The direct extension of that,
almost coaxial with a coil-coil

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:28.500 align:middle line:84%
[? or ?] protein-protein
interaction,

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:30.570 align:middle line:90%
they go down and touch the DNA.

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:33.750 align:middle line:84%
In contrast, the textbook where
it does the sharp right hand

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:41.070 align:middle line:84%
turn and in some way
poorly schematized there,

00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:42.690 align:middle line:90%
it goes coaxial to DNA.

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:45.030 align:middle line:90%
That's not what happens.

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:48.120 align:middle line:84%
The helices are more or
less direct extensions down

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:52.620 align:middle line:84%
from the dimerization
region of the protein

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:57.720 align:middle line:84%
maintaining almost
perpendicular to the DNA axis.

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:03.420 align:middle line:84%
But again, so on the left
is the three tandem repeats,

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:08.580 align:middle line:84%
and on the right is a dyad
axis, where the twofold 180

00:15:08.580 --> 00:15:10.320 align:middle line:90%
degrees symmetry--

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:13.890 align:middle line:84%
think of it as rotating
exactly 180 degrees.

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:14.790 align:middle line:90%
This is not a mirror.

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:16.140 align:middle line:90%
This is a rotation.

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:20.010 align:middle line:84%
Of the DNA on itself
also coincides perfectly

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:23.220 align:middle line:84%
with the twofold
symmetry of the protein

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:24.790 align:middle line:90%
association with itself.

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:26.850 align:middle line:84%
These are the two
major symmetry classes,

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:30.510 align:middle line:84%
and it's amazing how
many nucleic acid protein

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:33.330 align:middle line:84%
interactions fall into one
of these two classes-- direct

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:35.100 align:middle line:90%
repeat or inverted repeat.

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:37.710 align:middle line:84%
And that's the one you find
direct and inverted repeats

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:41.020 align:middle line:84%
in nucleic acid sequences,
you get a little excited.

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:45.120 align:middle line:84%
The other reason is
the hairpin structures

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:47.430 align:middle line:84%
that you found in RNA
that we talked about

00:15:47.430 --> 00:15:50.070 align:middle line:84%
in the last classes,
those also are

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:51.465 align:middle line:90%
indicated by inverted repeats.

00:15:51.465 --> 00:15:54.790 align:middle line:90%


00:15:54.790 --> 00:16:00.550 align:middle line:84%
So we now have a semi-empirical
way of computing--

00:16:00.550 --> 00:16:04.870 align:middle line:84%
in a certain sense, predicting
new regulatory protein

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:07.180 align:middle line:84%
DNA interactions with
double-stranded DNA.

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:08.920 align:middle line:90%
Can we extend this to RNA?

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:12.220 align:middle line:84%
This is a much more
complicated situation with RNA

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:15.140 align:middle line:84%
because you don't have these
long perfect double helices

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:15.640 align:middle line:90%
anymore.

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:17.740 align:middle line:84%
You have these very
short RNA helices

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:20.350 align:middle line:84%
that I showed in the
last couple of classes.

00:16:20.350 --> 00:16:22.930 align:middle line:84%
This is transfer RNA, one
of our favorite molecules

00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:24.910 align:middle line:84%
here, with the
anticodon at the bottom

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:26.830 align:middle line:90%
of each of the pink structures.

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:31.420 align:middle line:84%
And the amino acid acceptor
three prime end of that 70-some

00:16:31.420 --> 00:16:33.220 align:middle line:90%
nucleotide--

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:35.560 align:middle line:84%
70 to 80 nucleotide
nucleic acid.

00:16:35.560 --> 00:16:37.780 align:middle line:84%
So the pinks are all
the tRNAs, and there

00:16:37.780 --> 00:16:40.570 align:middle line:84%
are at least 20 different
types of amino acid

00:16:40.570 --> 00:16:43.870 align:middle line:84%
and has 20 types of-- at least
20 types of transfer RNAs

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:46.180 align:middle line:84%
and 20 types of
proteins that add

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:50.350 align:middle line:84%
the amino acid onto the three
prime ends of the transfer RNA.

00:16:50.350 --> 00:16:52.990 align:middle line:84%
These break themselves up
into two major classes.

00:16:52.990 --> 00:16:55.030 align:middle line:84%
These can be recognized
at the structural level.

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:58.660 align:middle line:84%
Class 1, which is the single
letter amino acid code

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:02.890 align:middle line:84%
[? CEL, ?] so forth, cysteine,
glutamate, and so on.

00:17:02.890 --> 00:17:05.170 align:middle line:84%
That's one class, which
are structurally similar.

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:08.770 align:middle line:84%
Class 2 is structurally
dissimilar to Class 1,

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:12.130 align:middle line:84%
but they are similar
within the class.

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:14.573 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, the point is
that they recognize

00:17:14.573 --> 00:17:16.240 align:middle line:84%
all different parts
of the nucleic acid,

00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:19.480 align:middle line:84%
not just the anticodon,
which is the code itself,

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:20.950 align:middle line:90%
the trinucleotide code.

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:22.810 align:middle line:84%
Not just the amino
acid end where

00:17:22.810 --> 00:17:26.050 align:middle line:84%
you need the
recognize amino acid,

00:17:26.050 --> 00:17:28.930 align:middle line:84%
but various points
along the transfer RNA.

00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:32.590 align:middle line:84%
If you wanted to create a new
code, as these authors have,

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:35.950 align:middle line:84%
or to create hybrids between
these various things,

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:40.270 align:middle line:84%
you'd have to find homology
among the proteins or graph

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:43.360 align:middle line:84%
domains of recognition
between each one

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:46.170 align:middle line:84%
or mutagenize
particular regions that

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:47.920 align:middle line:84%
are known to interact
with the nucleotides

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:51.250 align:middle line:84%
you want to contact,
and that's been done.

00:17:51.250 --> 00:17:53.260 align:middle line:84%
You can arrange to
make a new amino acid

00:17:53.260 --> 00:17:58.990 align:middle line:84%
by carving the pocket
the amino acid recognizes

00:17:58.990 --> 00:18:03.400 align:middle line:84%
and grafting on the
appropriate nucleotides--

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:05.950 align:middle line:84%
the appropriate amino
acids would recognize, say,

00:18:05.950 --> 00:18:08.200 align:middle line:90%
a stop codon.

00:18:08.200 --> 00:18:13.810 align:middle line:84%
OK, you've had some
programming experience

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:16.070 align:middle line:84%
that hopefully will prepare
you for the real world

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:21.600 align:middle line:84%
of interacting with input and
output from various devices.

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:25.500 align:middle line:90%


00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:27.660 align:middle line:84%
The topic today is
proteins, and this

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:32.730 align:middle line:84%
really is the main
contact between

00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:35.070 align:middle line:84%
the exquisite regulatory
mechanisms, which

00:18:35.070 --> 00:18:36.790 align:middle line:84%
will be the topic
of the [? network ?]

00:18:36.790 --> 00:18:38.790 align:middle line:84%
that we've already touched
upon, but will really

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:43.350 align:middle line:84%
be the topic of some
of our network analysis

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:45.990 align:middle line:90%
in the last three lectures.

00:18:45.990 --> 00:18:49.590 align:middle line:84%
Here, we need sensors to
sense the environment.

00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:51.720 align:middle line:84%
We need actuators
to then deliver back

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:54.870 align:middle line:84%
into the environment
what the cell wants to do

00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:56.880 align:middle line:90%
or to interact with other cells.

00:18:56.880 --> 00:18:59.910 align:middle line:84%
You have to have
feedback, synchrony,

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:04.170 align:middle line:84%
so on that you can
basically program the almost

00:19:04.170 --> 00:19:09.600 align:middle line:84%
digital nucleic acid
world inside the cell

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:14.475 align:middle line:84%
but via clearly analog
inputs and outputs.

00:19:14.475 --> 00:19:20.760 align:middle line:84%
So since this is the
Halloween lecture

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:26.160 align:middle line:84%
and I'm masquerading as
the Wolfman, we also--

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:32.350 align:middle line:84%
I've listed some of the scariest
proteins that I could think of.

00:19:32.350 --> 00:19:35.430 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to talk
about three of them.

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:39.720 align:middle line:84%
One of them in the slide,
which is the proteins that

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:42.210 align:middle line:84%
are actually involved in
causing the symptoms that

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:45.630 align:middle line:84%
come from when you're
worried about anthrax.

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:49.560 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll talk
about HIV yet again,

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:53.850 align:middle line:84%
this time, polymerase mutants
that cause drug resistance.

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:57.390 align:middle line:84%
And then ApoE yet again, as
we have in the past, this time

00:19:57.390 --> 00:20:00.630 align:middle line:84%
talking specifically about
how protein structure tells us

00:20:00.630 --> 00:20:01.690 align:middle line:90%
the haplotype.

00:20:01.690 --> 00:20:05.160 align:middle line:84%
So with anthrax, you start
out with this simple two

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:06.510 align:middle line:90%
component--

00:20:06.510 --> 00:20:08.760 align:middle line:90%
two protein domains here.

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:10.760 align:middle line:90%
They bind to a cellular--

00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:12.420 align:middle line:90%
something on your cell surface.

00:20:12.420 --> 00:20:15.780 align:middle line:84%
Hopefully not yours,
but human cell surface.

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:18.420 align:middle line:84%
And then one of the
domains disappears.

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:20.400 align:middle line:84%
And the remaining one
now self-assembles

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:23.310 align:middle line:84%
into a seven-mer,
seven-fold symmetry.

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:25.967 align:middle line:84%
Remember, we were talking about
two-fold rotational symmetry

00:20:25.967 --> 00:20:27.300 align:middle line:90%
for the DNA protein interaction.

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:29.430 align:middle line:84%
This is now seven-fold
rotational symmetry.

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:33.180 align:middle line:84%
That now allows lethal
factor, LF, to bind--

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:35.040 align:middle line:90%
still not inside the cell.

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:38.550 align:middle line:84%
But the whole complex
gets internalized.

00:20:38.550 --> 00:20:41.838 align:middle line:84%
Still, topologically, it's as
if it were outside the cell

00:20:41.838 --> 00:20:43.380 align:middle line:84%
when it's inside
this little vesicle.

00:20:43.380 --> 00:20:45.990 align:middle line:84%
It has to get through
that membrane.

00:20:45.990 --> 00:20:49.140 align:middle line:84%
But now the pH change that
happens when this vesicle goes

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:53.130 align:middle line:84%
in the cell, part of the natural
cell biological processes

00:20:53.130 --> 00:20:54.660 align:middle line:90%
causes some act--

00:20:54.660 --> 00:20:59.850 align:middle line:84%
an unfortunate act where,
now, the seven-mer complex

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:02.610 align:middle line:84%
of proteins does yet another
conformational change

00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:06.420 align:middle line:84%
and turns into this hairy beast
that allows the lethal factor

00:21:06.420 --> 00:21:08.290 align:middle line:84%
to get into your
cell and kill it.

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:10.290 align:middle line:84%
So you can see that
when we're talking

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:12.623 align:middle line:84%
about protein three-dimensional
structure, whether we're

00:21:12.623 --> 00:21:16.560 align:middle line:84%
predicting it or solving it,
protein is not a static object.

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:18.450 align:middle line:84%
Here, it associates
with one factor.

00:21:18.450 --> 00:21:21.150 align:middle line:90%
It associates seven of itself.

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:22.800 align:middle line:90%
It interacts with lethal factor.

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:26.730 align:middle line:84%
It opens up a whole new channel
in the membrane, et cetera.

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:28.635 align:middle line:84%
You need to think of
these as dynamic systems

00:21:28.635 --> 00:21:29.760 align:middle line:90%
with many different states.

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:32.570 align:middle line:90%


00:21:32.570 --> 00:21:35.420 align:middle line:84%
We also need to think
about time scales.

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:37.460 align:middle line:84%
Many of-- the
molecular mechanics

00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:40.250 align:middle line:84%
we'll be talking about,
the timescale of relevance

00:21:40.250 --> 00:21:41.660 align:middle line:90%
is the femtosecond.

00:21:41.660 --> 00:21:45.170 align:middle line:84%
You need to be able-- this
is, well, two nanoseconds.

00:21:45.170 --> 00:21:47.990 align:middle line:84%
So 10 to the minus 15th, 10
to the minus 9th seconds.

00:21:47.990 --> 00:21:49.730 align:middle line:90%
That's atomic motion.

00:21:49.730 --> 00:21:53.300 align:middle line:84%
The turnover of
an enzyme that is

00:21:53.300 --> 00:21:56.120 align:middle line:84%
the time it takes to
for a small molecule,

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:59.480 align:middle line:84%
say, to find and
bind the enzyme,

00:21:59.480 --> 00:22:02.750 align:middle line:84%
to possibly go through
a catalytic step,

00:22:02.750 --> 00:22:06.020 align:middle line:90%
and to dissociate as a product.

00:22:06.020 --> 00:22:08.540 align:middle line:84%
That's on the order of
microseconds to milliseconds.

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:12.760 align:middle line:84%
And the second range is the time
that it takes the molecule to--

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:15.650 align:middle line:84%
the drug or small molecule-- to
touch the surface of the cell,

00:22:15.650 --> 00:22:19.400 align:middle line:84%
maybe diffuse across the
cell, and find its target.

00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:21.020 align:middle line:84%
Transcription that
we talked about,

00:22:21.020 --> 00:22:26.000 align:middle line:84%
all of the regulatory mechanisms
of transcription last time,

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:30.170 align:middle line:84%
the rate of the constant
for that process

00:22:30.170 --> 00:22:34.760 align:middle line:84%
is around 50
nucleotides per second.

00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:36.320 align:middle line:84%
Not entirely
coincidentally, that's

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:40.220 align:middle line:84%
about the rate at which it
is translated into protein.

00:22:40.220 --> 00:22:44.210 align:middle line:84%
These are important numbers,
because a typical gene size

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:49.220 align:middle line:84%
piece, say, after RNA
splicing in higher organisms

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:52.820 align:middle line:84%
or naturally, it
might be a kilobase.

00:22:52.820 --> 00:22:56.600 align:middle line:84%
So that's about a half a minute
to transcribe and translate.

00:22:56.600 --> 00:22:59.300 align:middle line:90%


00:22:59.300 --> 00:23:03.830 align:middle line:84%
That could be used as a timer
in a circuit of these longer

00:23:03.830 --> 00:23:09.020 align:middle line:84%
time frames, like cell
cycle, circadian rhythm,

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:12.680 align:middle line:84%
very long time frames
in ecological systems

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:19.760 align:middle line:84%
with bamboo and various pests,
which can be not even yearly,

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:21.680 align:middle line:84%
and then development
and aging, which

00:23:21.680 --> 00:23:25.692 align:middle line:84%
can be on the order of hundreds
of years, at least for humans,

00:23:25.692 --> 00:23:26.525 align:middle line:90%
turtles, and whales.

00:23:26.525 --> 00:23:29.420 align:middle line:90%


00:23:29.420 --> 00:23:33.320 align:middle line:84%
So what we think
proteins are good for

00:23:33.320 --> 00:23:35.940 align:middle line:90%
depends on the accuracy.

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:37.790 align:middle line:84%
And the accuracy
depends on the method.

00:23:37.790 --> 00:23:44.490 align:middle line:84%
At the very bottom right, we
have a very appealing approach,

00:23:44.490 --> 00:23:50.480 align:middle line:84%
which is de novo, a
priori, or ab initio

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:52.873 align:middle line:84%
prediction of
secondary-- of protein

00:23:52.873 --> 00:23:55.040 align:middle line:84%
three-dimensional structure
from the sequence alone,

00:23:55.040 --> 00:23:56.457 align:middle line:84%
which we're getting
in bucketloads

00:23:56.457 --> 00:23:57.740 align:middle line:90%
from the genome projects.

00:23:57.740 --> 00:23:59.780 align:middle line:84%
But unfortunately,
accuracy so far--

00:23:59.780 --> 00:24:02.090 align:middle line:84%
and we'll delve into this
in more detail in a moment--

00:24:02.090 --> 00:24:05.300 align:middle line:84%
is on the order of six
angstroms of difference

00:24:05.300 --> 00:24:07.610 align:middle line:84%
between the predicted
structure and what it actually

00:24:07.610 --> 00:24:11.150 align:middle line:84%
is by the more precise
methods up higher on this.

00:24:11.150 --> 00:24:14.270 align:middle line:84%
The y-axis here,
the vertical axis,

00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:17.840 align:middle line:84%
is basically sequence identity
as you start doing, say,

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:22.550 align:middle line:84%
threading or comparative
modeling here as you get--

00:24:22.550 --> 00:24:26.420 align:middle line:84%
instead of the ab initio
or de novo prediction

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:28.400 align:middle line:84%
doesn't require any
sequence similarity.

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:31.370 align:middle line:84%
If you want to build it based
on previously solved structures,

00:24:31.370 --> 00:24:36.080 align:middle line:84%
you need at least 30%, but
you're still very far-- say,

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:37.850 align:middle line:90%
3 to 4 angstroms away--

00:24:37.850 --> 00:24:40.100 align:middle line:90%
from the native structure.

00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:43.280 align:middle line:84%
As you get to 1 Angstrom
or better in your accuracy,

00:24:43.280 --> 00:24:46.040 align:middle line:84%
as you can get from NMR
and X-ray crystallography,

00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:49.460 align:middle line:84%
you now are in a position
to study catalytic mechanism

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:52.130 align:middle line:84%
and design and improve
ligands, such as drugs.

00:24:52.130 --> 00:24:53.870 align:middle line:84%
This is really
where we want to be.

00:24:53.870 --> 00:24:57.380 align:middle line:84%
There may be a day where we
can do this all from ab initio

00:24:57.380 --> 00:24:59.900 align:middle line:84%
prediction or modeling
at very great distances.

00:24:59.900 --> 00:25:03.170 align:middle line:84%
But for now, modeling
at very short,

00:25:03.170 --> 00:25:07.070 align:middle line:84%
say, 80% to 90% amino acid
similarity, is important.

00:25:07.070 --> 00:25:09.440 align:middle line:84%
Remember, there's
a-- just like there's

00:25:09.440 --> 00:25:13.710 align:middle line:84%
a variety of different
protein structures.

00:25:13.710 --> 00:25:17.180 align:middle line:84%
This is just an example
of a vast literature that

00:25:17.180 --> 00:25:21.350 align:middle line:84%
exists where you can
use some of the methods

00:25:21.350 --> 00:25:27.140 align:middle line:84%
that we'll be
discussing in this class

00:25:27.140 --> 00:25:31.640 align:middle line:84%
on doing molecular
mechanics on proteins

00:25:31.640 --> 00:25:34.220 align:middle line:84%
and predicting their
three-dimensional structure

00:25:34.220 --> 00:25:37.880 align:middle line:84%
in complex with
the various drugs.

00:25:37.880 --> 00:25:40.250 align:middle line:84%
We will contrast this,
or show the interplay

00:25:40.250 --> 00:25:41.900 align:middle line:84%
of the computational
biology, that

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:45.560 align:middle line:84%
can be aided by actual
measurements of drug binding,

00:25:45.560 --> 00:25:48.290 align:middle line:84%
just as we had actual
measurements of zinc finger

00:25:48.290 --> 00:25:50.480 align:middle line:90%
binding to double-stranded DNA.

00:25:50.480 --> 00:25:53.780 align:middle line:84%
And ways that you can
discover the small molecules

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:57.845 align:middle line:84%
by a clever use of
parts of it that you

00:25:57.845 --> 00:26:00.660 align:middle line:84%
know bind and parts
of it that you know

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:06.260 align:middle line:84%
might be variable
in a chemical sense.

00:26:06.260 --> 00:26:07.970 align:middle line:90%
Now, this is what--

00:26:07.970 --> 00:26:13.340 align:middle line:84%
just as there is this dynamic
competition between pathogens

00:26:13.340 --> 00:26:18.560 align:middle line:84%
and their hosts, there's
this similar lethal game

00:26:18.560 --> 00:26:21.470 align:middle line:84%
that's played between pathogens
and the pharmaceutical

00:26:21.470 --> 00:26:22.340 align:middle line:90%
industry.

00:26:22.340 --> 00:26:26.120 align:middle line:84%
And here, HIV, for which
there are many drugs now aimed

00:26:26.120 --> 00:26:29.723 align:middle line:84%
either at the protease
or at the polymerase--

00:26:29.723 --> 00:26:31.890 align:middle line:84%
some of the first ones were
aimed at the polymerase,

00:26:31.890 --> 00:26:34.010 align:middle line:90%
and so we have a big collection.

00:26:34.010 --> 00:26:36.230 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the most
sequenced molecules on Earth,

00:26:36.230 --> 00:26:40.700 align:middle line:84%
which is the HIV gene encoding
the reverse transcriptase

00:26:40.700 --> 00:26:41.690 align:middle line:90%
polymerase.

00:26:41.690 --> 00:26:43.250 align:middle line:84%
And it's been
sequenced many times

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:45.110 align:middle line:84%
because as a patient
takes the drugs,

00:26:45.110 --> 00:26:48.510 align:middle line:84%
their population of
the AIDS virus changes.

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:53.420 align:middle line:84%
And each of these little
diamond-shaped substitution

00:26:53.420 --> 00:26:56.375 align:middle line:84%
sites clustered around the
binding site in the protein--

00:26:56.375 --> 00:26:58.250 align:middle line:84%
the binding site here
indicated the substrate

00:26:58.250 --> 00:27:01.070 align:middle line:84%
is in space filling,
which is the triphosphate

00:27:01.070 --> 00:27:05.930 align:middle line:84%
on the upper left
and that the template

00:27:05.930 --> 00:27:08.540 align:middle line:84%
kind of curving around on the
right-hand side of that space

00:27:08.540 --> 00:27:12.680 align:middle line:90%
filling bright green structure.

00:27:12.680 --> 00:27:15.138 align:middle line:90%
The protein is in red.

00:27:15.138 --> 00:27:17.180 align:middle line:84%
And these little diamonds
indicate substitutions,

00:27:17.180 --> 00:27:20.120 align:middle line:84%
where the nomenclature is
single-letter code for the wild

00:27:20.120 --> 00:27:25.970 align:middle line:84%
type, the position in amino
acids from the end terminus is

00:27:25.970 --> 00:27:29.330 align:middle line:90%
the number, and then the third--

00:27:29.330 --> 00:27:33.330 align:middle line:84%
or the last far right letter
is the new amino acids.

00:27:33.330 --> 00:27:37.490 align:middle line:84%
So for example, D67N means a
[? sparcade ?] at position 67

00:27:37.490 --> 00:27:40.070 align:middle line:84%
and wild type changes
to an asparagine.

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:44.240 align:middle line:84%
And that causes a drug
resistance in the HIV,

00:27:44.240 --> 00:27:46.775 align:middle line:84%
with unfortunate
consequences for the patient.

00:27:46.775 --> 00:27:52.240 align:middle line:90%


00:27:52.240 --> 00:27:58.110 align:middle line:84%
We can take-- now, making
mutations in polymerases

00:27:58.110 --> 00:28:01.390 align:middle line:84%
is not entirely of
negative consequences.

00:28:01.390 --> 00:28:04.740 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to show you a
really beautiful example where

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:09.030 align:middle line:84%
a DNA polymerase-- a very
similar kind of dynamic,

00:28:09.030 --> 00:28:13.320 align:middle line:84%
where the DNA polymerase, you
want to change it so that it

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:19.740 align:middle line:84%
can now handle what would
normally be an inhibitor of DNA

00:28:19.740 --> 00:28:27.990 align:middle line:84%
polymerase, a class of
nucleotides that is incapable

00:28:27.990 --> 00:28:29.310 align:middle line:90%
of extending--

00:28:29.310 --> 00:28:34.770 align:middle line:84%
capable of being incorporated
into the growing replicating

00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:38.160 align:middle line:84%
genome, but is not
capable of extending.

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:40.680 align:middle line:84%
Is a powerful inhibitor,
and it's also powerful

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:43.110 align:middle line:84%
sequencing reagent,
these dideoxys.

00:28:43.110 --> 00:28:48.450 align:middle line:84%
And so one of the
things that was noticed

00:28:48.450 --> 00:28:52.203 align:middle line:84%
as the sequences of some
of these polymerases

00:28:52.203 --> 00:28:54.120 align:middle line:84%
were being studied, and
some of the resistance

00:28:54.120 --> 00:28:56.910 align:middle line:84%
mutants, and so
on, it was noticed

00:28:56.910 --> 00:29:03.060 align:middle line:84%
that the complex between
the nucleotide, whether it's

00:29:03.060 --> 00:29:07.830 align:middle line:84%
a deoxynucleotide shown here
with a 3 prime hydroxyl, which

00:29:07.830 --> 00:29:11.790 align:middle line:84%
could then be extended
by bringing in the next 5

00:29:11.790 --> 00:29:13.410 align:middle line:90%
phosphate.

00:29:13.410 --> 00:29:17.610 align:middle line:84%
This hydroxyl is near
in space to the position

00:29:17.610 --> 00:29:20.190 align:middle line:84%
on a phenylalanine
or a tyrosine,

00:29:20.190 --> 00:29:24.840 align:middle line:84%
position 762 of this
polymerase, which can either--

00:29:24.840 --> 00:29:28.988 align:middle line:84%
if it's a tyrosine,
it has an OH there.

00:29:28.988 --> 00:29:30.780 align:middle line:84%
And if it's a phenylalanine,
you lose the O

00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:32.700 align:middle line:84%
and you just have
a hydrogen there.

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:38.550 align:middle line:84%
And when you have the
phenylalanine there,

00:29:38.550 --> 00:29:42.060 align:middle line:90%
there's a space that--

00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:45.210 align:middle line:84%
an appropriate space
that accommodates the 3

00:29:45.210 --> 00:29:46.810 align:middle line:90%
prime hydroxyl quite well.

00:29:46.810 --> 00:29:49.500 align:middle line:84%
But if you now put in
a dideoxy inhibitor,

00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:51.840 align:middle line:84%
you now have too
much space in there,

00:29:51.840 --> 00:29:54.330 align:middle line:84%
and you start trying
to fill that space

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:58.050 align:middle line:84%
with other bulky
molecules, like water.

00:29:58.050 --> 00:30:02.190 align:middle line:84%
And basically, the
binding constant,

00:30:02.190 --> 00:30:04.770 align:middle line:84%
it becomes much less
favorable binding

00:30:04.770 --> 00:30:07.960 align:middle line:84%
when you're lacking
both oxygens.

00:30:07.960 --> 00:30:12.970 align:middle line:84%
So this presented an opportunity
to engineer some polymerases

00:30:12.970 --> 00:30:18.510 align:middle line:84%
which had a phenylalanine
there to become

00:30:18.510 --> 00:30:21.960 align:middle line:84%
more accepting of the
dideoxys and hence better

00:30:21.960 --> 00:30:25.650 align:middle line:84%
at using disease in DNA
sequencing chemistry.

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:27.810 align:middle line:84%
And this was simply
by engineering--

00:30:27.810 --> 00:30:30.450 align:middle line:84%
putting in that oxygen there,
by changing the phenylalanine

00:30:30.450 --> 00:30:35.790 align:middle line:84%
to a tyrosine, it now made
a better fit between--

00:30:35.790 --> 00:30:38.355 align:middle line:90%
you can think of it as--

00:30:38.355 --> 00:30:42.330 align:middle line:84%
you can have either oxygen,
and by removing this oxygen,

00:30:42.330 --> 00:30:47.730 align:middle line:84%
you replace it with an
oxygen on the protein side.

00:30:47.730 --> 00:30:50.130 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to emphasize,
by a few examples here,

00:30:50.130 --> 00:30:52.020 align:middle line:84%
the idea of
complementary surfaces

00:30:52.020 --> 00:30:53.530 align:middle line:90%
and how you can engineer them.

00:30:53.530 --> 00:30:54.600 align:middle line:90%
This is a beautiful case.

00:30:54.600 --> 00:30:56.910 align:middle line:84%
Now, we're talking about
a single atom rather than

00:30:56.910 --> 00:30:59.010 align:middle line:84%
the complementary surfaces
of the nucleic acids we

00:30:59.010 --> 00:31:00.480 align:middle line:90%
were talking about before.

00:31:00.480 --> 00:31:04.530 align:middle line:84%
This has an 8,000-fold effect
on the specificity of this

00:31:04.530 --> 00:31:08.610 align:middle line:84%
polymerase, and a big impact
on the Genome Project.

00:31:08.610 --> 00:31:11.610 align:middle line:84%
Now, that's how we
program a particular atom

00:31:11.610 --> 00:31:13.740 align:middle line:90%
to achieve an important goal.

00:31:13.740 --> 00:31:15.870 align:middle line:84%
And of course, the virus
has its own mechanisms

00:31:15.870 --> 00:31:18.995 align:middle line:84%
for programming it typically,
by random mutagenesis

00:31:18.995 --> 00:31:20.370 align:middle line:84%
and selection, as
we talked about

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:21.570 align:middle line:90%
in the population genetics.

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:24.240 align:middle line:84%
But the way we program
in general proteins

00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:30.840 align:middle line:84%
are either transgenics, where we
might overproduce the protein,

00:31:30.840 --> 00:31:32.580 align:middle line:84%
or homologous
recombination, which

00:31:32.580 --> 00:31:35.813 align:middle line:84%
is the ultimate where we go in
and if the protein is already--

00:31:35.813 --> 00:31:37.980 align:middle line:84%
if the gene encoding the
protein is already present,

00:31:37.980 --> 00:31:40.710 align:middle line:84%
we can change that
particular nucleotide in situ

00:31:40.710 --> 00:31:43.020 align:middle line:84%
in the correct place so
it's properly regulated

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:43.740 align:middle line:90%
and everything.

00:31:43.740 --> 00:31:47.160 align:middle line:90%
That's a great way to do it.

00:31:47.160 --> 00:31:49.037 align:middle line:84%
Point mutants are
not the only way

00:31:49.037 --> 00:31:50.370 align:middle line:90%
to generate conditional mutants.

00:31:50.370 --> 00:31:51.662 align:middle line:90%
Many of them historically were.

00:31:51.662 --> 00:31:53.820 align:middle line:84%
But there are ways
that you can program,

00:31:53.820 --> 00:31:56.610 align:middle line:84%
and conditional, meaning that
you can regulate under what

00:31:56.610 --> 00:31:58.470 align:middle line:84%
conditions the
protein is expressed

00:31:58.470 --> 00:32:02.250 align:middle line:84%
or not or active or not
with an entire domain,

00:32:02.250 --> 00:32:05.970 align:middle line:84%
or with single
nucleotide polymorphisms.

00:32:05.970 --> 00:32:07.440 align:middle line:90%
Now, so this is one way.

00:32:07.440 --> 00:32:11.110 align:middle line:90%
This is the nucleic acid way.

00:32:11.110 --> 00:32:15.870 align:middle line:84%
Another way is by modulating
the activity of the proteins

00:32:15.870 --> 00:32:19.680 align:middle line:84%
from the outside with drugs
or drug-like molecules

00:32:19.680 --> 00:32:21.210 align:middle line:90%
and chemical kinetics.

00:32:21.210 --> 00:32:23.280 align:middle line:84%
And under the
subheadings for that,

00:32:23.280 --> 00:32:25.770 align:middle line:84%
you can make these by
combinatorial synthesis--

00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:27.240 align:middle line:84%
and we'll show an
example of that.

00:32:27.240 --> 00:32:30.990 align:middle line:84%
But combinatorial synthesis can
be based on design principles,

00:32:30.990 --> 00:32:33.450 align:middle line:90%
not just completely random.

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:34.058 align:middle line:90%
Usually are.

00:32:34.058 --> 00:32:35.850 align:middle line:84%
The design principles
can take into account

00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:38.400 align:middle line:84%
what you know about the
nature of the interaction

00:32:38.400 --> 00:32:41.790 align:middle line:90%
of similar proteins.

00:32:41.790 --> 00:32:49.740 align:middle line:84%
And you can mine
whatever biochemical data

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:52.420 align:middle line:84%
that you can collect for
so-called quantitative

00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:53.950 align:middle line:84%
structure activity
relationships.

00:32:53.950 --> 00:32:56.560 align:middle line:84%
This is a slightly
different discipline

00:32:56.560 --> 00:33:02.447 align:middle line:84%
than the detailed
crystallographic

00:33:02.447 --> 00:33:04.780 align:middle line:84%
and quantitative studies that
we've talked about so far.

00:33:04.780 --> 00:33:07.360 align:middle line:84%
Here, you're trying
to basically mine

00:33:07.360 --> 00:33:11.320 align:middle line:84%
through the structures
of the ligand itself

00:33:11.320 --> 00:33:13.450 align:middle line:84%
for the parts of the
ligand that might

00:33:13.450 --> 00:33:15.610 align:middle line:84%
be responsible for the
activity, the binding

00:33:15.610 --> 00:33:20.090 align:middle line:84%
activity or the full biological
activity that you see.

00:33:20.090 --> 00:33:23.320 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at some
examples of single-nucleotide

00:33:23.320 --> 00:33:27.610 align:middle line:84%
polymorphisms that we've been
talking about before in--

00:33:27.610 --> 00:33:32.440 align:middle line:84%
actually, this is a class
that we didn't discuss before.

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:34.690 align:middle line:90%
But in previous classes.

00:33:34.690 --> 00:33:37.450 align:middle line:84%
But it's related to what
we've been talking about.

00:33:37.450 --> 00:33:40.233 align:middle line:84%
In the case of the zinc finger,
we made an altered specificity.

00:33:40.233 --> 00:33:41.650 align:middle line:84%
We made new zinc
fingers with bind

00:33:41.650 --> 00:33:44.500 align:middle line:84%
to completely new
trinucleotides.

00:33:44.500 --> 00:33:48.310 align:middle line:84%
With the DNA polymerase,
by changing one amino acid,

00:33:48.310 --> 00:33:52.450 align:middle line:84%
we could make it now accept
almost four logs better

00:33:52.450 --> 00:33:54.920 align:middle line:90%
an inhibitor is very useful.

00:33:54.920 --> 00:33:58.480 align:middle line:84%
And here, many
different-- many of these

00:33:58.480 --> 00:34:03.520 align:middle line:84%
are enzymes, where you can
not just knock out the enzyme,

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:06.190 align:middle line:84%
but actually make it
recognize a new substrate,

00:34:06.190 --> 00:34:11.679 align:middle line:84%
or change radically the binding
constant and catalytic rate

00:34:11.679 --> 00:34:13.120 align:middle line:90%
for new substrates.

00:34:13.120 --> 00:34:16.270 align:middle line:90%


00:34:16.270 --> 00:34:19.219 align:middle line:84%
And I just have this long
fine-print slide just

00:34:19.219 --> 00:34:21.580 align:middle line:84%
to impress to you-- this
is actually less than half

00:34:21.580 --> 00:34:23.320 align:middle line:90%
of the list--

00:34:23.320 --> 00:34:24.469 align:middle line:90%
just how many examples.

00:34:24.469 --> 00:34:26.320 align:middle line:90%
These are not that unusual.

00:34:26.320 --> 00:34:30.909 align:middle line:84%
And those can be designed
or naturally occurring.

00:34:30.909 --> 00:34:34.900 align:middle line:84%
Now, we're going to take the
three-dimensional structure

00:34:34.900 --> 00:34:44.860 align:middle line:84%
of proteins and connect it with
our discussion of haplotypes

00:34:44.860 --> 00:34:46.870 align:middle line:84%
and single-nucleotide
polymorphisms.

00:34:46.870 --> 00:34:51.100 align:middle line:84%
And you may recall
that with-- one

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:55.270 align:middle line:84%
of the commonly occurring
single-nucleotide

00:34:55.270 --> 00:34:59.890 align:middle line:84%
polymorphisms is
the ApoE4 allele.

00:34:59.890 --> 00:35:02.020 align:middle line:84%
It's present in 20% of
the human population.

00:35:02.020 --> 00:35:06.430 align:middle line:84%
Even though it has unfortunate
consequences, we think,

00:35:06.430 --> 00:35:10.480 align:middle line:90%
mainly for Alzheimer's--

00:35:10.480 --> 00:35:13.360 align:middle line:84%
it increases the risk of
Alzheimer's, and probably

00:35:13.360 --> 00:35:20.770 align:middle line:84%
increases cardiovascular
fitness through ApoE refers

00:35:20.770 --> 00:35:24.790 align:middle line:84%
to its involvement in
cholesterol metabolism

00:35:24.790 --> 00:35:25.960 align:middle line:90%
and transport.

00:35:25.960 --> 00:35:28.870 align:middle line:84%
The ApoE3 allele is
present in about 80%,

00:35:28.870 --> 00:35:31.810 align:middle line:84%
and is far more common in human
populations, but both of these

00:35:31.810 --> 00:35:34.370 align:middle line:84%
would be considered
very common alleles.

00:35:34.370 --> 00:35:37.510 align:middle line:84%
And we also mentioned that
the ancestral form of this,

00:35:37.510 --> 00:35:40.990 align:middle line:84%
for example, found in
chimpanzees at nearly 100%,

00:35:40.990 --> 00:35:44.980 align:middle line:84%
is this arginine 112,
instead of what's now common

00:35:44.980 --> 00:35:47.170 align:middle line:84%
in human populations
was cysteine 112.

00:35:47.170 --> 00:35:50.080 align:middle line:84%
And that was-- one explanation
for that might be that it was

00:35:50.080 --> 00:35:52.990 align:middle line:90%
physiologically--

00:35:52.990 --> 00:35:55.760 align:middle line:84%
our nutritional
standards have changed.

00:35:55.760 --> 00:35:57.430 align:middle line:84%
We now eat a lot
more fatty things.

00:35:57.430 --> 00:35:59.570 align:middle line:84%
We live long enough
to get Alzheimer's.

00:35:59.570 --> 00:36:04.150 align:middle line:84%
And so maybe this was
something that was--

00:36:04.150 --> 00:36:07.480 align:middle line:84%
this bad allele, E4, was
good in chimpanzees that have

00:36:07.480 --> 00:36:09.940 align:middle line:90%
different diets or lifespans.

00:36:09.940 --> 00:36:13.870 align:middle line:84%
But the other possibility--
and I can't really

00:36:13.870 --> 00:36:15.850 align:middle line:84%
distinguish between
these right now--

00:36:15.850 --> 00:36:17.770 align:middle line:84%
but another one to
seriously consider,

00:36:17.770 --> 00:36:19.990 align:middle line:84%
not just in this case,
but in cases in general,

00:36:19.990 --> 00:36:23.620 align:middle line:84%
is you no longer just think
about single nucleotides.

00:36:23.620 --> 00:36:24.910 align:middle line:90%
You think about haplotypes.

00:36:24.910 --> 00:36:27.970 align:middle line:84%
Everything insists
on that DNA strand

00:36:27.970 --> 00:36:29.950 align:middle line:84%
has a chance of affecting
either the expression

00:36:29.950 --> 00:36:33.580 align:middle line:84%
level of the protein or
insists, on the protein strand,

00:36:33.580 --> 00:36:35.560 align:middle line:90%
to fall back and interact.

00:36:35.560 --> 00:36:38.230 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that one
of the nearest amino acids

00:36:38.230 --> 00:36:41.770 align:middle line:84%
to this arginine 112, which
is the main difference

00:36:41.770 --> 00:36:44.710 align:middle line:90%
between ApoE4 and ApoE3.

00:36:44.710 --> 00:36:47.200 align:middle line:84%
Arginine 61 is the same
on the two alleles.

00:36:47.200 --> 00:36:50.320 align:middle line:84%
But you think of this
as one haplotype,

00:36:50.320 --> 00:36:53.830 align:middle line:84%
and in chimpanzees the
haplotype, is now threonine 61.

00:36:53.830 --> 00:36:59.620 align:middle line:84%
And you can think that
a [? 3R ?] in chimps,

00:36:59.620 --> 00:37:05.560 align:middle line:84%
or ancestrally, is not too
different from [? Rcys. ?] So

00:37:05.560 --> 00:37:07.250 align:middle line:90%
the different order.

00:37:07.250 --> 00:37:10.510 align:middle line:84%
So it's like this compensating,
complementary mutation,

00:37:10.510 --> 00:37:16.480 align:middle line:84%
just like we had in the
oxygens in the polymerase

00:37:16.480 --> 00:37:17.710 align:middle line:90%
a couple of slides ago.

00:37:17.710 --> 00:37:19.752 align:middle line:84%
And you can think of the
compensating mutations--

00:37:19.752 --> 00:37:22.340 align:middle line:84%
we had the mutual information
theory for doing the theory

00:37:22.340 --> 00:37:23.230 align:middle line:90%
structure.

00:37:23.230 --> 00:37:24.700 align:middle line:90%
Think of complementary surfaces.

00:37:24.700 --> 00:37:27.880 align:middle line:84%
When you think of
single nucleotides,

00:37:27.880 --> 00:37:33.070 align:middle line:84%
don't think of them as haplotype
and possibly complementing

00:37:33.070 --> 00:37:34.930 align:middle line:90%
constellations.

00:37:34.930 --> 00:37:40.690 align:middle line:84%
Especially, now, this brings
us to the possible impact

00:37:40.690 --> 00:37:42.610 align:middle line:84%
of three-dimensional
structure on predicting

00:37:42.610 --> 00:37:43.900 align:middle line:90%
deleterious human alleles.

00:37:43.900 --> 00:37:46.900 align:middle line:84%
If we suddenly had the sequence
of everyone in this room

00:37:46.900 --> 00:37:50.548 align:middle line:84%
and we wanted our computer
program to prioritize, which

00:37:50.548 --> 00:37:51.840 align:middle line:90%
ones should I the attention to?

00:37:51.840 --> 00:37:56.190 align:middle line:84%
Which deviations from
the most common allele

00:37:56.190 --> 00:37:57.212 align:middle line:90%
should I look at first?

00:37:57.212 --> 00:37:59.670 align:middle line:84%
Well, you might think of these
things in terms of proteins.

00:37:59.670 --> 00:38:01.470 align:middle line:84%
We've now gotten to
the point in the course

00:38:01.470 --> 00:38:03.030 align:middle line:84%
where we're talking
about proteins.

00:38:03.030 --> 00:38:05.363 align:middle line:84%
You need to think about the
three-dimensional structure.

00:38:05.363 --> 00:38:07.770 align:middle line:90%
Who's near who in the structure?

00:38:07.770 --> 00:38:09.300 align:middle line:84%
You can think about
binding sites.

00:38:09.300 --> 00:38:10.980 align:middle line:90%
These might be indicated by--

00:38:10.980 --> 00:38:14.250 align:middle line:84%
if you know the
three-dimensional structure

00:38:14.250 --> 00:38:16.350 align:middle line:84%
or you know the
conservation pattern

00:38:16.350 --> 00:38:18.450 align:middle line:90%
in this family of proteins.

00:38:18.450 --> 00:38:21.300 align:middle line:90%
You can ask things about charge.

00:38:21.300 --> 00:38:22.950 align:middle line:84%
In that last slide,
we had the charge

00:38:22.950 --> 00:38:28.500 align:middle line:84%
of the arginines being
near a compatibly

00:38:28.500 --> 00:38:35.100 align:middle line:84%
partial negative charge on
the cysteines or threonines.

00:38:35.100 --> 00:38:40.050 align:middle line:84%
You can have-- a disulfide is
a very important thing to lose.

00:38:40.050 --> 00:38:43.410 align:middle line:84%
They tend to be
highly conserved.

00:38:43.410 --> 00:38:47.220 align:middle line:84%
If you introduce a proline into
what would normally be an alpha

00:38:47.220 --> 00:38:49.223 align:middle line:84%
helix, this is something
where knowledge

00:38:49.223 --> 00:38:50.640 align:middle line:84%
of the three-dimensional
structure

00:38:50.640 --> 00:38:53.940 align:middle line:84%
would say, oh, that proline,
this a priori, without any

00:38:53.940 --> 00:38:56.310 align:middle line:84%
knowledge of
conservation, could be

00:38:56.310 --> 00:39:00.240 align:middle line:84%
a huge change in the
three-dimensional structure.

00:39:00.240 --> 00:39:02.730 align:middle line:84%
And then these
multi-sequence profiles

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:06.810 align:middle line:84%
are a good way of looking
at the conservation.

00:39:06.810 --> 00:39:10.830 align:middle line:84%
That's a way of prioritizing
single-nucleotide polymorphisms

00:39:10.830 --> 00:39:14.040 align:middle line:84%
that might have impact on
pharmacogenomics or disease

00:39:14.040 --> 00:39:15.240 align:middle line:90%
in general.

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:19.920 align:middle line:84%
Now, as we integrate that
with the chemical diversity

00:39:19.920 --> 00:39:20.772 align:middle line:90%
that we can create--

00:39:20.772 --> 00:39:22.980 align:middle line:84%
that's going to be the topic
for the next few slides,

00:39:22.980 --> 00:39:26.730 align:middle line:84%
is how do we create
chemical diversity?

00:39:26.730 --> 00:39:30.570 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to introduce this,
the idea of chemical diversity,

00:39:30.570 --> 00:39:33.540 align:middle line:84%
in a way I hope nicely
connects to where

00:39:33.540 --> 00:39:36.030 align:middle line:90%
we've been with RNA arrays.

00:39:36.030 --> 00:39:38.820 align:middle line:84%
RNA arrays, and the
double-stranded RNA array

00:39:38.820 --> 00:39:41.790 align:middle line:84%
that we used earlier
in class today,

00:39:41.790 --> 00:39:43.680 align:middle line:84%
can be generated in
a commentorial sense.

00:39:43.680 --> 00:39:45.750 align:middle line:90%
You can make an exhaustive set.

00:39:45.750 --> 00:39:48.780 align:middle line:84%
Now, typically, those were
made where, spatially, they

00:39:48.780 --> 00:39:49.590 align:middle line:90%
were isolated.

00:39:49.590 --> 00:39:55.650 align:middle line:84%
Each different nucleic
acid, oligonucleotide,

00:39:55.650 --> 00:39:58.800 align:middle line:84%
is present in a different place
identifiable to the computer

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:01.780 align:middle line:90%
by its coordinates on an array.

00:40:01.780 --> 00:40:05.550 align:middle line:84%
But you can also make
them in a big mixture

00:40:05.550 --> 00:40:08.610 align:middle line:84%
and use them as a mixture and
do selection on them, as we

00:40:08.610 --> 00:40:10.800 align:middle line:90%
did with the phage display.

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:14.490 align:middle line:84%
Or you can make them as
a mixture of solid phase

00:40:14.490 --> 00:40:17.670 align:middle line:84%
particles and then separate the
[INAUDIBLE] phase particles out

00:40:17.670 --> 00:40:19.350 align:middle line:90%
in some manner.

00:40:19.350 --> 00:40:21.765 align:middle line:84%
Solid phase comes up
again and again in arrays.

00:40:21.765 --> 00:40:23.640 align:middle line:84%
It's very obvious why
you have a solid phase.

00:40:23.640 --> 00:40:27.360 align:middle line:84%
You want to be able to address
it by its positions in x and y

00:40:27.360 --> 00:40:29.700 align:middle line:90%
on the array.

00:40:29.700 --> 00:40:31.470 align:middle line:84%
But the other reason--
technical reasons

00:40:31.470 --> 00:40:33.930 align:middle line:84%
are, it's a fantastic way
of getting purification

00:40:33.930 --> 00:40:36.990 align:middle line:84%
of your products
simply by washing

00:40:36.990 --> 00:40:40.290 align:middle line:84%
rather than doing complicated
purification procedures.

00:40:40.290 --> 00:40:43.110 align:middle line:84%
And it allows you to,
in the case of beads,

00:40:43.110 --> 00:40:45.750 align:middle line:84%
there now-- you can think of
it as an ultimate and flexible

00:40:45.750 --> 00:40:46.380 align:middle line:90%
array.

00:40:46.380 --> 00:40:50.460 align:middle line:84%
You can move the beads around
and put them in new arrays,

00:40:50.460 --> 00:40:53.250 align:middle line:90%
and identify them later.

00:40:53.250 --> 00:40:57.870 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so we're going to
introduce the general way

00:40:57.870 --> 00:41:01.230 align:middle line:90%
of making either--

00:41:01.230 --> 00:41:03.660 align:middle line:84%
complex chemicals,
whether they're

00:41:03.660 --> 00:41:07.440 align:middle line:84%
linear polymers like
proteins or nucleic acids,

00:41:07.440 --> 00:41:15.150 align:middle line:84%
or much more tighter
and small molecules.

00:41:15.150 --> 00:41:17.093 align:middle line:84%
But they have similar
concepts that you need.

00:41:17.093 --> 00:41:19.260 align:middle line:84%
There's the solid phase
that I already talked about.

00:41:19.260 --> 00:41:23.760 align:middle line:84%
There's the idea of
protecting groups,

00:41:23.760 --> 00:41:25.650 align:middle line:84%
and the protecting
groups are protecting

00:41:25.650 --> 00:41:27.030 align:middle line:90%
against a reactive group.

00:41:27.030 --> 00:41:28.980 align:middle line:84%
So the highly
reactive group here

00:41:28.980 --> 00:41:33.090 align:middle line:84%
is the phosphoramidite, which
is this phosphate nitrogen bond.

00:41:33.090 --> 00:41:39.480 align:middle line:84%
This is capable of reacting
with just about any nitrogen

00:41:39.480 --> 00:41:41.400 align:middle line:84%
or oxygen, such as
this-- eventually,

00:41:41.400 --> 00:41:44.400 align:middle line:84%
once you do protect this
oxygen at the 5 prime position,

00:41:44.400 --> 00:41:46.702 align:middle line:84%
these two oxygens are 5 and
3, just like the ones we've

00:41:46.702 --> 00:41:47.910 align:middle line:90%
been talking about all along.

00:41:47.910 --> 00:41:51.150 align:middle line:84%
This is the chemical synthesis
version of the polymerase

00:41:51.150 --> 00:41:52.560 align:middle line:90%
that we've been talking about.

00:41:52.560 --> 00:41:54.600 align:middle line:84%
So you have these
reactive groups

00:41:54.600 --> 00:41:57.660 align:middle line:90%
and the protecting groups.

00:41:57.660 --> 00:41:59.910 align:middle line:84%
And those are the
major concepts.

00:41:59.910 --> 00:42:01.140 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's go through.

00:42:01.140 --> 00:42:08.250 align:middle line:84%
This is-- the topic
here is proteins.

00:42:08.250 --> 00:42:11.610 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk more
about protein synthesis

00:42:11.610 --> 00:42:14.435 align:middle line:84%
as part of
quantitation next time,

00:42:14.435 --> 00:42:16.560 align:middle line:84%
and as part of networks in
the last three lectures.

00:42:16.560 --> 00:42:18.480 align:middle line:84%
But here's a completely
synthetic way

00:42:18.480 --> 00:42:21.780 align:middle line:84%
of getting short
peptides, either

00:42:21.780 --> 00:42:23.550 align:middle line:84%
by directly synthesizing
the peptides

00:42:23.550 --> 00:42:27.300 align:middle line:84%
or synthesizing nucleic acid
that encodes that peptide

00:42:27.300 --> 00:42:29.490 align:middle line:84%
or interferes with the
production of that peptide.

00:42:29.490 --> 00:42:32.430 align:middle line:84%
And you can think of these
as drug-like molecules.

00:42:32.430 --> 00:42:35.100 align:middle line:90%
These are naturally related--

00:42:35.100 --> 00:42:37.570 align:middle line:84%
they can be analogs of
nucleic acids and proteins,

00:42:37.570 --> 00:42:39.563 align:middle line:90%
not just straight ones.

00:42:39.563 --> 00:42:40.980 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk
about opportunities

00:42:40.980 --> 00:42:43.530 align:middle line:90%
for making these analogs.

00:42:43.530 --> 00:42:47.100 align:middle line:84%
And so by making analogs
of known proteins

00:42:47.100 --> 00:42:51.230 align:middle line:84%
or nucleic acids, you kind
of have a more immediate

00:42:51.230 --> 00:42:54.440 align:middle line:84%
connection between the thing
that your computer instructed

00:42:54.440 --> 00:42:57.440 align:middle line:84%
the synthesizer to
make and your targets.

00:42:57.440 --> 00:42:59.622 align:middle line:84%
Well, if you make
a random chemical,

00:42:59.622 --> 00:43:01.580 align:middle line:84%
you don't necessarily
know what your target is.

00:43:01.580 --> 00:43:03.320 align:middle line:84%
But we'll talk
about ways of making

00:43:03.320 --> 00:43:05.150 align:middle line:90%
slightly less random chemicals.

00:43:05.150 --> 00:43:07.640 align:middle line:84%
But this is one way of
making a direct connection.

00:43:07.640 --> 00:43:11.150 align:middle line:84%
And the process is
cyclic in the sense

00:43:11.150 --> 00:43:15.260 align:middle line:84%
that each cycle, you
return, and the polymer

00:43:15.260 --> 00:43:16.370 align:middle line:90%
gets a little bit longer.

00:43:16.370 --> 00:43:19.580 align:middle line:84%
You start with one monomer
on a solid phase, shown

00:43:19.580 --> 00:43:25.940 align:middle line:84%
by these little hexagons on the
far right side of the slide.

00:43:25.940 --> 00:43:30.830 align:middle line:84%
And you add-- you remove
the protecting group

00:43:30.830 --> 00:43:34.670 align:middle line:84%
on the immobilized polymer,
one protecting group.

00:43:34.670 --> 00:43:36.530 align:middle line:84%
And then you bring in
this reactive group,

00:43:36.530 --> 00:43:38.240 align:middle line:90%
otherwise protected.

00:43:38.240 --> 00:43:41.210 align:middle line:84%
And there's really one major
product that you expect.

00:43:41.210 --> 00:43:42.860 align:middle line:90%
You wash off all of the excess.

00:43:42.860 --> 00:43:44.540 align:middle line:90%
You now have one longer.

00:43:44.540 --> 00:43:45.410 align:middle line:90%
You deprotect.

00:43:45.410 --> 00:43:48.440 align:middle line:90%
This DMT group is removed.

00:43:48.440 --> 00:43:51.080 align:middle line:84%
And you go back up
and cycle again.

00:43:51.080 --> 00:43:54.300 align:middle line:84%
There may be additional
steps, such as oxidation,

00:43:54.300 --> 00:43:57.290 align:middle line:84%
which will stabilize the
new bond that you've made.

00:43:57.290 --> 00:44:02.840 align:middle line:84%
Or you can have a capping
step that can soak up

00:44:02.840 --> 00:44:05.332 align:middle line:90%
any excess that was left over.

00:44:05.332 --> 00:44:07.790 align:middle line:84%
But in general, after you're
done with all of these cycles,

00:44:07.790 --> 00:44:10.100 align:middle line:84%
then there'll be
a step where you

00:44:10.100 --> 00:44:12.800 align:middle line:84%
remove the protecting
groups altogether and remove

00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:15.830 align:middle line:84%
the polymer from the solid
phase if you so choose.

00:44:15.830 --> 00:44:18.500 align:middle line:84%
Or you leave it there,
if you have an array.

00:44:18.500 --> 00:44:21.590 align:middle line:84%
These are other examples
of protecting groups.

00:44:21.590 --> 00:44:23.030 align:middle line:90%
These are now on the bases.

00:44:23.030 --> 00:44:25.940 align:middle line:84%
Some bases don't need
protection, like thiamines.

00:44:25.940 --> 00:44:27.830 align:middle line:84%
If you have an exocyclic
amine, then you

00:44:27.830 --> 00:44:32.120 align:middle line:84%
typically need a benzyl or an
isobutyryl group in blue here,

00:44:32.120 --> 00:44:33.275 align:middle line:90%
are the protecting groups.

00:44:33.275 --> 00:44:37.100 align:middle line:90%


00:44:37.100 --> 00:44:44.750 align:middle line:84%
I said there's an opportunity
here for modifying

00:44:44.750 --> 00:44:52.160 align:middle line:84%
the nucleotides or
oligopeptides or other chemicals

00:44:52.160 --> 00:44:56.270 align:middle line:84%
to make them so that
they're related to, but not

00:44:56.270 --> 00:45:01.400 align:middle line:84%
identical in every property,
to normal constituents

00:45:01.400 --> 00:45:06.200 align:middle line:84%
of your body or of a bacterial
cell that you're aiming at.

00:45:06.200 --> 00:45:08.210 align:middle line:84%
And why would you want
to make derivatives?

00:45:08.210 --> 00:45:09.680 align:middle line:90%
Why not make the exact thing?

00:45:09.680 --> 00:45:10.940 align:middle line:84%
And the reason you want
to make derivatives

00:45:10.940 --> 00:45:13.100 align:middle line:84%
would be, for example, to
increase their stability,

00:45:13.100 --> 00:45:19.100 align:middle line:84%
or decrease their stability,
or make them bind more faster

00:45:19.100 --> 00:45:20.510 align:middle line:90%
or more irreversibly.

00:45:20.510 --> 00:45:23.150 align:middle line:84%
And examples are, in
the previous slide,

00:45:23.150 --> 00:45:24.710 align:middle line:90%
you can make modified bases.

00:45:24.710 --> 00:45:28.460 align:middle line:84%
And in slide 29 here you can
change the backbone itself.

00:45:28.460 --> 00:45:30.290 align:middle line:84%
You can change the
ribosomes so that they

00:45:30.290 --> 00:45:34.430 align:middle line:84%
have bulky groups that prevent
the nucleases from getting in,

00:45:34.430 --> 00:45:41.150 align:middle line:84%
or they can exchange oxygen
for sulfurs or hydrogens

00:45:41.150 --> 00:45:44.520 align:middle line:84%
in order to make the
phosphodiester backbone itself,

00:45:44.520 --> 00:45:47.030 align:middle line:84%
which is where
nucleus is cleaved,

00:45:47.030 --> 00:45:51.200 align:middle line:84%
a less attractive,
less energetically

00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:54.200 align:middle line:90%
favorable substrate.

00:45:54.200 --> 00:45:57.990 align:middle line:84%
So now, those are
chemical processes

00:45:57.990 --> 00:46:01.340 align:middle line:84%
that, in a certain sense,
mimic the normal polymerases

00:46:01.340 --> 00:46:03.680 align:middle line:90%
and ribosomes in the cell.

00:46:03.680 --> 00:46:07.100 align:middle line:84%
And there are analogous
processes to generate chemical

00:46:07.100 --> 00:46:10.580 align:middle line:90%
diversity on smaller molecules.

00:46:10.580 --> 00:46:14.810 align:middle line:84%
And then there are,
analogous to that,

00:46:14.810 --> 00:46:19.910 align:middle line:84%
biological mechanisms by which
you can make small molecule

00:46:19.910 --> 00:46:25.460 align:middle line:84%
diversity which are less
cyclic than processes

00:46:25.460 --> 00:46:26.720 align:middle line:90%
we just talked about.

00:46:26.720 --> 00:46:34.580 align:middle line:84%
These are more a set
of ordered reactions

00:46:34.580 --> 00:46:38.460 align:middle line:84%
that has a conceptual
repeat, but in a sense,

00:46:38.460 --> 00:46:40.970 align:middle line:84%
you can think of it
as a linear program

00:46:40.970 --> 00:46:43.710 align:middle line:84%
that you go from the
beginning to the end.

00:46:43.710 --> 00:46:46.880 align:middle line:84%
And that's to make
these polyketides, which

00:46:46.880 --> 00:46:49.010 align:middle line:84%
are shown on the right-hand
side of the slide.

00:46:49.010 --> 00:46:53.060 align:middle line:84%
A large class of
pharmaceuticals,

00:46:53.060 --> 00:46:54.950 align:middle line:84%
including most of
the antibiotics,

00:46:54.950 --> 00:46:57.860 align:middle line:84%
are made by a fairly
small set of organisms,

00:46:57.860 --> 00:47:00.260 align:middle line:84%
such as streptomyces
in certain plants.

00:47:00.260 --> 00:47:02.572 align:middle line:84%
And this process
by which it's made,

00:47:02.572 --> 00:47:05.030 align:middle line:84%
which will be illustrated in
more detail in the next slide,

00:47:05.030 --> 00:47:07.520 align:middle line:84%
is very akin to
fatty acid synthesis.

00:47:07.520 --> 00:47:09.830 align:middle line:84%
Fatty acids are long
hydrocarbon chains,

00:47:09.830 --> 00:47:12.860 align:middle line:84%
which you can think
of, adding each two

00:47:12.860 --> 00:47:15.710 align:middle line:84%
carbons on that fatty
acid is a process

00:47:15.710 --> 00:47:20.270 align:middle line:84%
akin to the one for making these
polyketide drug-like molecules.

00:47:20.270 --> 00:47:22.580 align:middle line:84%
Another way of
biologically making

00:47:22.580 --> 00:47:28.160 align:middle line:84%
a very compact structure,
which actually uses ribosomes,

00:47:28.160 --> 00:47:33.440 align:middle line:84%
but it uses them to make
very tight short peptides

00:47:33.440 --> 00:47:37.040 align:middle line:84%
and a precursor that then
folds with lots of disulfides

00:47:37.040 --> 00:47:41.750 align:middle line:84%
to make this small and
highly cross-linked.

00:47:41.750 --> 00:47:47.210 align:middle line:84%
Now, the fact that these
cone snails have gone

00:47:47.210 --> 00:47:49.040 align:middle line:84%
have gone to the trouble
of making hundreds

00:47:49.040 --> 00:47:52.010 align:middle line:84%
of these different very
small peptides that

00:47:52.010 --> 00:47:55.730 align:middle line:84%
have these properties tells
you something about what

00:47:55.730 --> 00:47:59.630 align:middle line:84%
it is that you want-- that
drug-like molecules have

00:47:59.630 --> 00:48:00.230 align:middle line:90%
in common.

00:48:00.230 --> 00:48:06.080 align:middle line:84%
And that is, they're small, so
they diffuse quickly and get

00:48:06.080 --> 00:48:07.230 align:middle line:90%
to their site.

00:48:07.230 --> 00:48:09.020 align:middle line:84%
And so you can have
large amounts of them

00:48:09.020 --> 00:48:10.050 align:middle line:90%
in a small space.

00:48:10.050 --> 00:48:12.110 align:middle line:84%
So you can manufacture
lots of them.

00:48:12.110 --> 00:48:15.680 align:middle line:84%
And then they have to be
highly cross-linked in order

00:48:15.680 --> 00:48:17.060 align:middle line:90%
to maintain the rigidity.

00:48:17.060 --> 00:48:19.730 align:middle line:84%
Because they're small,
they have less surface area

00:48:19.730 --> 00:48:23.640 align:middle line:90%
to bind to their binding pocket.

00:48:23.640 --> 00:48:26.810 align:middle line:84%
So to compensate for that, they
have to have a lot of rigidity.

00:48:26.810 --> 00:48:29.210 align:middle line:84%
And the thing that
you lock in rigidly

00:48:29.210 --> 00:48:30.740 align:middle line:90%
has to be the correct structure.

00:48:30.740 --> 00:48:32.930 align:middle line:84%
It does you no good to
have a rigid structure that

00:48:32.930 --> 00:48:36.330 align:middle line:84%
isn't really perfectly
complementary to that surface.

00:48:36.330 --> 00:48:40.070 align:middle line:84%
And the third source
of biological diversity

00:48:40.070 --> 00:48:41.820 align:middle line:84%
is one you're probably
more familiar with,

00:48:41.820 --> 00:48:44.510 align:middle line:84%
which is the immune
receptors, the B and T cell

00:48:44.510 --> 00:48:49.680 align:middle line:84%
receptors, the antibodies,
and cell-mediated immunity.

00:48:49.680 --> 00:48:57.200 align:middle line:84%
And these use
recombination machinery

00:48:57.200 --> 00:49:04.940 align:middle line:84%
to program various combinations
of nucleic acid motifs

00:49:04.940 --> 00:49:06.290 align:middle line:90%
that encode protein motifs.

00:49:06.290 --> 00:49:08.030 align:middle line:84%
And as they do
that recombination,

00:49:08.030 --> 00:49:10.130 align:middle line:84%
they have further
diversification

00:49:10.130 --> 00:49:12.740 align:middle line:84%
that occurs due to a
template-independent

00:49:12.740 --> 00:49:15.290 align:middle line:84%
polymerase,
[? thermotransferase, ?]

00:49:15.290 --> 00:49:18.050 align:middle line:84%
which will extend a few
nucleotides of completely

00:49:18.050 --> 00:49:23.630 align:middle line:84%
random nucleic acid sequence
that basically incredibly

00:49:23.630 --> 00:49:26.690 align:middle line:84%
accelerates the rate of
mutagenesis, basically

00:49:26.690 --> 00:49:28.130 align:middle line:90%
generating sequence de novo.

00:49:28.130 --> 00:49:30.590 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the
examples in biology

00:49:30.590 --> 00:49:33.050 align:middle line:84%
where you generate
sequence de novo.

00:49:33.050 --> 00:49:40.040 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's very apropos
of this combinatorial topic.

00:49:40.040 --> 00:49:44.030 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is a beautiful
example from the previous slide.

00:49:44.030 --> 00:49:45.800 align:middle line:84%
Those polyketides
on the far right

00:49:45.800 --> 00:49:48.300 align:middle line:90%
now are the star of this slide.

00:49:48.300 --> 00:49:50.960 align:middle line:84%
And here, in a certain
sense, nature has--

00:49:50.960 --> 00:49:54.470 align:middle line:84%
and now scientists have--
engineered protein modules

00:49:54.470 --> 00:49:59.240 align:middle line:84%
to make this linear
sequence of events.

00:49:59.240 --> 00:50:03.200 align:middle line:84%
You can think of, here,
you're using a linear set

00:50:03.200 --> 00:50:07.280 align:middle line:84%
of protein domains to program
very complicated series

00:50:07.280 --> 00:50:09.650 align:middle line:84%
of chemical reactions, the
same way the linear sequence

00:50:09.650 --> 00:50:12.530 align:middle line:84%
of messenger RNA
tells the ribosome

00:50:12.530 --> 00:50:16.680 align:middle line:84%
to make a series of
additions in the protein.

00:50:16.680 --> 00:50:21.230 align:middle line:84%
The ribosome catalytic
cycle is a cycle,

00:50:21.230 --> 00:50:24.200 align:middle line:84%
while this is more
a linear tape,

00:50:24.200 --> 00:50:26.390 align:middle line:90%
a linear series of events.

00:50:26.390 --> 00:50:29.000 align:middle line:84%
The proteins themselves, these
little arrow-shaped things

00:50:29.000 --> 00:50:32.150 align:middle line:84%
with boxes in them
along the top,

00:50:32.150 --> 00:50:35.960 align:middle line:84%
labeled Module 1 through 6,
those proteins are, of course,

00:50:35.960 --> 00:50:36.860 align:middle line:90%
made on ribosomes.

00:50:36.860 --> 00:50:40.520 align:middle line:84%
But then they act kind of like
the solid phase synthesis,

00:50:40.520 --> 00:50:43.160 align:middle line:84%
where the acyl carrier
protein, ACP in the box,

00:50:43.160 --> 00:50:46.070 align:middle line:84%
binds to the first
monomer, and it

00:50:46.070 --> 00:50:48.710 align:middle line:84%
starts transferring
it from protein

00:50:48.710 --> 00:50:53.977 align:middle line:84%
to protein along this
multi-domain huge protein.

00:50:53.977 --> 00:50:56.060 align:middle line:84%
And there's actually three
proteins in a row here.

00:50:56.060 --> 00:50:59.120 align:middle line:84%
And each of the steps are taken
in order along the protein,

00:50:59.120 --> 00:51:02.210 align:middle line:84%
and they involve things like a
synthase step, where you bring

00:51:02.210 --> 00:51:06.290 align:middle line:84%
in another monomer, or a
keto reductase step, KR,

00:51:06.290 --> 00:51:08.540 align:middle line:90%
where you'll bring in--

00:51:08.540 --> 00:51:13.340 align:middle line:84%
where you'll reduce one
of the double bonds,

00:51:13.340 --> 00:51:16.910 align:middle line:90%
or an acyltransferase step, AT.

00:51:16.910 --> 00:51:20.990 align:middle line:90%


00:51:20.990 --> 00:51:24.020 align:middle line:84%
But you can see each of these
has a substrate specificity.

00:51:24.020 --> 00:51:27.470 align:middle line:84%
And by changing the order
of substrate specificities,

00:51:27.470 --> 00:51:31.700 align:middle line:84%
you can build up a huge
combinatorial collection here

00:51:31.700 --> 00:51:38.570 align:middle line:84%
in microbial communities,
and also in the laboratory.

00:51:38.570 --> 00:51:40.220 align:middle line:90%
Now, protein interaction.

00:51:40.220 --> 00:51:43.580 align:middle line:84%
We're just beginning to
talk seriously about protein

00:51:43.580 --> 00:51:45.680 align:middle line:90%
interaction assays.

00:51:45.680 --> 00:51:48.710 align:middle line:84%
I think many of you
either are or will

00:51:48.710 --> 00:51:52.850 align:middle line:84%
be more and more familiar with
the protein interaction assays.

00:51:52.850 --> 00:51:56.570 align:middle line:84%
In next class, we'll
talk about ways

00:51:56.570 --> 00:51:59.400 align:middle line:84%
of getting direct
information on protein

00:51:59.400 --> 00:52:02.210 align:middle line:84%
through cross-linking
and mass spectrometry.

00:52:02.210 --> 00:52:04.370 align:middle line:84%
Another way is
indirectly setting up

00:52:04.370 --> 00:52:06.500 align:middle line:84%
these reporter
assays, where you take

00:52:06.500 --> 00:52:09.530 align:middle line:84%
advantage of the binding
properties of known proteins

00:52:09.530 --> 00:52:12.410 align:middle line:90%
to analyze two unknown proteins.

00:52:12.410 --> 00:52:14.930 align:middle line:84%
And so a known protein
might be, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:52:14.930 --> 00:52:19.070 align:middle line:84%
which binds to DNA, and B42,
which activates transcription

00:52:19.070 --> 00:52:23.630 align:middle line:84%
of something for which you
have a good visual assay,

00:52:23.630 --> 00:52:27.560 align:middle line:90%
like [? URO3, ?] life and death.

00:52:27.560 --> 00:52:30.800 align:middle line:84%
And by taking these
two knowns, let's say,

00:52:30.800 --> 00:52:33.200 align:middle line:84%
in B42, which have
known properties--

00:52:33.200 --> 00:52:35.562 align:middle line:84%
but they only exert
their properties

00:52:35.562 --> 00:52:37.520 align:middle line:84%
when they're brought
together, and they're only

00:52:37.520 --> 00:52:40.220 align:middle line:84%
brought together if the
unknown proteins or partially

00:52:40.220 --> 00:52:44.460 align:middle line:84%
known proteins to which they're
bound interact with each other.

00:52:44.460 --> 00:52:47.450 align:middle line:84%
And so this is a
so-called two-hybrid assay

00:52:47.450 --> 00:52:49.920 align:middle line:90%
and variations on it.

00:52:49.920 --> 00:52:52.460 align:middle line:84%
And I think we
mentioned one where

00:52:52.460 --> 00:52:55.220 align:middle line:84%
you can characterize nucleic
acid-protein interactions

00:52:55.220 --> 00:52:56.600 align:middle line:90%
with one hybrid assay.

00:52:56.600 --> 00:53:00.680 align:middle line:84%
And here, you can
inhibit this interaction

00:53:00.680 --> 00:53:03.590 align:middle line:90%
between the two knowns--

00:53:03.590 --> 00:53:07.970 align:middle line:84%
sorry, unknowns or partially
known molecules in blue here.

00:53:07.970 --> 00:53:16.040 align:middle line:84%
Here's a TGF beta, a growth
factor, and a binding protein.

00:53:16.040 --> 00:53:19.880 align:middle line:84%
You can inhibit that with
a particular small molecule

00:53:19.880 --> 00:53:21.860 align:middle line:84%
or a collection of
small molecules, which

00:53:21.860 --> 00:53:25.580 align:middle line:84%
can be introduced from one of
these combinatorial syntheses

00:53:25.580 --> 00:53:29.660 align:middle line:84%
into an array of
these cellular assays.

00:53:29.660 --> 00:53:34.040 align:middle line:84%
And you get this
information about--

00:53:34.040 --> 00:53:36.020 align:middle line:84%
you can either
collect a big data

00:53:36.020 --> 00:53:39.500 align:middle line:84%
set of proteins that interact
from a proteomic scale

00:53:39.500 --> 00:53:42.920 align:middle line:84%
experiment, or from molecules
that inhibit one or more

00:53:42.920 --> 00:53:44.550 align:middle line:90%
of those interactions.

00:53:44.550 --> 00:53:47.240 align:middle line:84%
This is a source of
information, which

00:53:47.240 --> 00:53:50.292 align:middle line:84%
is intrinsically computational
in the sense-- well,

00:53:50.292 --> 00:53:52.250 align:middle line:84%
in the sense that there's
a large amount of it.

00:53:52.250 --> 00:53:54.620 align:middle line:84%
You can model the
three-dimensional structure

00:53:54.620 --> 00:53:57.600 align:middle line:84%
of this interaction if you have
sufficient data to do that.

00:53:57.600 --> 00:54:00.740 align:middle line:84%
And you can model the impact
of the small molecules

00:54:00.740 --> 00:54:03.410 align:middle line:84%
in a structure
activity [? since. ?]

00:54:03.410 --> 00:54:08.390 align:middle line:84%
Now, if you look at the top
right-hand part of slide 34

00:54:08.390 --> 00:54:10.790 align:middle line:84%
here, you can see
this huge diversity

00:54:10.790 --> 00:54:12.690 align:middle line:84%
of all of these
different colored shapes.

00:54:12.690 --> 00:54:17.570 align:middle line:84%
And if you wanted to use these
in a combinatorial assay,

00:54:17.570 --> 00:54:20.960 align:middle line:84%
you'd connect them in
every pairwise combination

00:54:20.960 --> 00:54:24.530 align:middle line:84%
and try them against your
target by some bioassay.

00:54:24.530 --> 00:54:28.280 align:middle line:84%
However, if that
library is too large

00:54:28.280 --> 00:54:29.930 align:middle line:90%
either to make or to screen--

00:54:29.930 --> 00:54:31.880 align:middle line:84%
typically, to screen--
then what you can do

00:54:31.880 --> 00:54:35.880 align:middle line:84%
is, you can study a
part of the molecule

00:54:35.880 --> 00:54:39.960 align:middle line:84%
and see-- and then take the
subset of the diversity that

00:54:39.960 --> 00:54:47.130 align:middle line:84%
can bind, and then
take that subset,

00:54:47.130 --> 00:54:50.760 align:middle line:84%
characterize it, and now make
only the pairwise combinations

00:54:50.760 --> 00:54:53.470 align:middle line:90%
of the two half-molecules.

00:54:53.470 --> 00:55:00.720 align:middle line:84%
And generally speaking, if
the geometry is fairly rigid,

00:55:00.720 --> 00:55:05.040 align:middle line:84%
then the binding
constant will get--

00:55:05.040 --> 00:55:08.230 align:middle line:84%
will be roughly the product
of the two binding constants.

00:55:08.230 --> 00:55:10.890 align:middle line:84%
So if each one has a very
low binding constant,

00:55:10.890 --> 00:55:14.830 align:middle line:84%
then it will be roughly
the square of that.

00:55:14.830 --> 00:55:18.780 align:middle line:84%
And you get some point
of diminishing returns,

00:55:18.780 --> 00:55:21.210 align:middle line:90%
eventually.

00:55:21.210 --> 00:55:24.240 align:middle line:84%
So this is an example
of a strategy where

00:55:24.240 --> 00:55:27.900 align:middle line:84%
you use a little bit of
prior knowledge, which

00:55:27.900 --> 00:55:32.460 align:middle line:84%
can be empirical or it could be
purely computational, about how

00:55:32.460 --> 00:55:36.030 align:middle line:84%
to limit your library and
make interesting combinations.

00:55:36.030 --> 00:55:39.860 align:middle line:90%


00:55:39.860 --> 00:55:42.800 align:middle line:84%
Now, we've been talking mainly
about the kind of chemical

00:55:42.800 --> 00:55:45.350 align:middle line:84%
diversity we can get that's
aimed at the ligands that

00:55:45.350 --> 00:55:46.970 align:middle line:90%
combine to target proteins.

00:55:46.970 --> 00:55:50.780 align:middle line:84%
But now, we want to talk about
the source of information

00:55:50.780 --> 00:55:52.860 align:middle line:84%
about those target
proteins itself,

00:55:52.860 --> 00:56:01.250 align:middle line:84%
which is another genomics
project, structural genomics.

00:56:01.250 --> 00:56:07.250 align:middle line:84%
And typically, we want to select
targets for binding drugs,

00:56:07.250 --> 00:56:10.760 align:middle line:84%
or select targets for solving
the structures of proteins

00:56:10.760 --> 00:56:14.720 align:middle line:84%
in order to look at their
ligands in more detail.

00:56:14.720 --> 00:56:17.900 align:middle line:90%
And how can we do this?

00:56:17.900 --> 00:56:20.830 align:middle line:84%
How can we decide which
targets are high on our list

00:56:20.830 --> 00:56:21.850 align:middle line:90%
to go for next?

00:56:21.850 --> 00:56:23.650 align:middle line:84%
We have hundreds of
proteins for which we

00:56:23.650 --> 00:56:25.240 align:middle line:84%
have three-dimensional
structures,

00:56:25.240 --> 00:56:29.770 align:middle line:84%
and from some of them,
we have information

00:56:29.770 --> 00:56:32.530 align:middle line:90%
on what ligands they bind.

00:56:32.530 --> 00:56:34.210 align:middle line:84%
But these are
other criteria that

00:56:34.210 --> 00:56:38.170 align:middle line:84%
are sometimes used in the
field for target selection.

00:56:38.170 --> 00:56:44.050 align:middle line:84%
If they are homologous to
previous interesting targets,

00:56:44.050 --> 00:56:48.190 align:middle line:84%
then that puts them
high on the short list.

00:56:48.190 --> 00:56:50.380 align:middle line:84%
If they are conserved--
and we've talked about how

00:56:50.380 --> 00:56:53.620 align:middle line:84%
important conservation
is from time to time--

00:56:53.620 --> 00:56:56.650 align:middle line:90%
then that might be an approach.

00:56:56.650 --> 00:57:01.640 align:middle line:84%
If they're conserved
and you knock it out,

00:57:01.640 --> 00:57:03.640 align:middle line:84%
then you might expect
that to be lethal.

00:57:03.640 --> 00:57:10.510 align:middle line:84%
And that might make it a good
target for an anti-bacterial.

00:57:10.510 --> 00:57:18.700 align:middle line:84%
If you want to limit the action
of your therapy to the surface

00:57:18.700 --> 00:57:23.980 align:middle line:84%
in order to, say, reduce
the cross-reaction

00:57:23.980 --> 00:57:28.270 align:middle line:84%
with internal molecules,
you can sometimes

00:57:28.270 --> 00:57:31.360 align:middle line:84%
restrict yourself to the
surface-acceptable proteins.

00:57:31.360 --> 00:57:34.000 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, a very
large class of drugs

00:57:34.000 --> 00:57:39.500 align:middle line:84%
is aimed at surface-accessible
membrane proteins.

00:57:39.500 --> 00:57:42.100 align:middle line:84%
And so very often, those
are prioritized high.

00:57:42.100 --> 00:57:44.590 align:middle line:84%
There are also-- the
surface-acceptable proteins are

00:57:44.590 --> 00:57:47.720 align:middle line:84%
important if you're
talking about vaccines,

00:57:47.720 --> 00:57:50.500 align:middle line:84%
which is an
increasingly important--

00:57:50.500 --> 00:57:55.330 align:middle line:84%
or diagnostics that
are non-invasive,

00:57:55.330 --> 00:57:59.620 align:middle line:84%
or at least not going to
the interior of the cell.

00:57:59.620 --> 00:58:02.110 align:middle line:84%
And there are ways,
say, with microarrays,

00:58:02.110 --> 00:58:04.180 align:middle line:84%
that you can ask which
genes are differentially

00:58:04.180 --> 00:58:05.590 align:middle line:90%
expressed in the disease state.

00:58:05.590 --> 00:58:07.780 align:middle line:84%
And that causes
high prioritization.

00:58:07.780 --> 00:58:10.750 align:middle line:84%
Now, once you have that
prioritization, which

00:58:10.750 --> 00:58:13.720 align:middle line:84%
comes from, say,
genome sequencing

00:58:13.720 --> 00:58:17.680 align:middle line:84%
and some of those
other facts, then we

00:58:17.680 --> 00:58:19.570 align:middle line:84%
would like to have--
you've got your target.

00:58:19.570 --> 00:58:21.400 align:middle line:84%
You've got your
genome sequence--

00:58:21.400 --> 00:58:24.340 align:middle line:90%


00:58:24.340 --> 00:58:26.152 align:middle line:90%
gene sequence for that target.

00:58:26.152 --> 00:58:28.360 align:middle line:84%
How, then, do you get the
three-dimensional structure

00:58:28.360 --> 00:58:32.680 align:middle line:84%
that helps you design
drugs or improve

00:58:32.680 --> 00:58:34.330 align:middle line:90%
the drugs that you have?

00:58:34.330 --> 00:58:42.280 align:middle line:84%
Well, one very
attractive approach,

00:58:42.280 --> 00:58:44.920 align:middle line:84%
given a protein
sequence which might

00:58:44.920 --> 00:58:48.430 align:middle line:84%
get from their deluge
of genome sequences,

00:58:48.430 --> 00:58:52.150 align:middle line:84%
the practical approach
might be to start

00:58:52.150 --> 00:58:54.370 align:middle line:84%
with this genome sequence--
this gene sequence which

00:58:54.370 --> 00:58:59.410 align:middle line:84%
is 99.99% accurate, and try to
predict the three dimensional

00:58:59.410 --> 00:59:02.410 align:middle line:84%
structure of the protein
and its ligand specificity.

00:59:02.410 --> 00:59:04.105 align:middle line:84%
And if you walk
through these, these

00:59:04.105 --> 00:59:05.980 align:middle line:84%
are kind of ballpark
estimates, some of them

00:59:05.980 --> 00:59:07.060 align:middle line:90%
better than others.

00:59:07.060 --> 00:59:08.680 align:middle line:84%
To get from the
sequence to exons--

00:59:08.680 --> 00:59:10.120 align:middle line:90%
we talked about this before--

00:59:10.120 --> 00:59:12.065 align:middle line:90%
might be an 80%.

00:59:12.065 --> 00:59:14.440 align:middle line:84%
Remember, these numbers really
should be false positives,

00:59:14.440 --> 00:59:16.160 align:middle line:90%
false negatives, so on.

00:59:16.160 --> 00:59:21.670 align:middle line:84%
But this ballpark, 80%, getting
to exons, exons to genes.

00:59:21.670 --> 00:59:25.660 align:middle line:84%
If you aren't privileged
enough to have the cDNA,

00:59:25.660 --> 00:59:29.800 align:middle line:84%
then this is an
error-prone process

00:59:29.800 --> 00:59:34.120 align:middle line:90%
with maybe a 30% success rate.

00:59:34.120 --> 00:59:36.795 align:middle line:84%
Once you have genes
knowing it's regulation,

00:59:36.795 --> 00:59:38.920 align:middle line:84%
whether it's on or off in
the particular cell types

00:59:38.920 --> 00:59:43.090 align:middle line:84%
you're interested in, is
very difficult right now.

00:59:43.090 --> 00:59:47.410 align:middle line:84%
Knowing the motifs
is barely a start

00:59:47.410 --> 00:59:48.950 align:middle line:90%
on getting the full regulation.

00:59:48.950 --> 00:59:51.670 align:middle line:90%
So I would say 10% or less.

00:59:51.670 --> 00:59:54.280 align:middle line:84%
Once you have a regulated gene,
getting the protein sequence

00:59:54.280 --> 00:59:55.030 align:middle line:90%
is easy.

00:59:55.030 --> 00:59:56.380 align:middle line:90%
That's the genetic code.

00:59:56.380 --> 00:59:59.110 align:middle line:84%
Getting from the sequence
to secondary structure

00:59:59.110 --> 01:00:03.520 align:middle line:84%
is easy in the context of
some of these other things,

01:00:03.520 --> 01:00:06.880 align:middle line:84%
but still, the accuracy
is only around 77%.

01:00:06.880 --> 01:00:10.240 align:middle line:84%
I have next to this the
reference is [? cast, ?]

01:00:10.240 --> 01:00:13.540 align:middle line:84%
which is something
that is a competition

01:00:13.540 --> 01:00:16.420 align:middle line:84%
for computational assessment
of three-dimensional structure

01:00:16.420 --> 01:00:20.110 align:middle line:84%
prediction for proteins that's
been held since, I think, '94,

01:00:20.110 --> 01:00:22.750 align:middle line:84%
and the next one is coming
up in a couple of months.

01:00:22.750 --> 01:00:26.270 align:middle line:84%
And this is kind of the
big race or bake-off

01:00:26.270 --> 01:00:27.520 align:middle line:90%
between the different methods.

01:00:27.520 --> 01:00:28.810 align:middle line:90%
Very exciting.

01:00:28.810 --> 01:00:31.080 align:middle line:84%
But unfortunately,
over decades, it's

01:00:31.080 --> 01:00:34.210 align:middle line:84%
still hovering around 77%
for secondary structure

01:00:34.210 --> 01:00:39.010 align:middle line:84%
and about 25% for ab initio
three-dimensional structure.

01:00:39.010 --> 01:00:41.560 align:middle line:84%
Then even if you have the
three-dimensional structure

01:00:41.560 --> 01:00:45.250 align:middle line:84%
at adequate accuracy, getting
the ligand specificity

01:00:45.250 --> 01:00:48.760 align:middle line:84%
is problematic, and it
depends on the ligand.

01:00:48.760 --> 01:00:50.620 align:middle line:90%
If it's DNA, it's a good case.

01:00:50.620 --> 01:00:54.580 align:middle line:84%
If it's a small molecule, it
can be as low as 10% or worse.

01:00:54.580 --> 01:00:56.500 align:middle line:84%
Now, since each of
these are fairly

01:00:56.500 --> 01:01:01.600 align:middle line:84%
independent of
estimates, you can

01:01:01.600 --> 01:01:04.940 align:middle line:84%
get an approximate
overall accuracy

01:01:04.940 --> 01:01:06.850 align:middle line:84%
which is a product of
all of those, which

01:01:06.850 --> 01:01:13.400 align:middle line:90%
is dismally small at 0.0005.

01:01:13.400 --> 01:01:18.490 align:middle line:84%
And so it behooves one to use
as many extra-experimental data

01:01:18.490 --> 01:01:21.580 align:middle line:84%
as one can, or improve
the algorithms that

01:01:21.580 --> 01:01:25.780 align:middle line:84%
are weakest in this journey
from genome sequence

01:01:25.780 --> 01:01:28.870 align:middle line:90%
to ligand specificity.

01:01:28.870 --> 01:01:33.190 align:middle line:84%
We'll pick up this thread
right after a break

01:01:33.190 --> 01:01:37.420 align:middle line:84%
and carry on to
actually how we get

01:01:37.420 --> 01:01:40.720 align:middle line:84%
the three-dimensional
structure, whether it's

01:01:40.720 --> 01:01:45.190 align:middle line:84%
predictive or experimental, and
the computational tasks there.

01:01:45.190 --> 01:01:46.570 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

01:01:46.570 --> 01:01:48.360 align:middle line:90%
Take a break.

01:01:48.360 --> 01:01:49.000 align:middle line:90%